/* generated by tools/i18n_build.py — 19 chapters, 17 templates. Edit tools/i18n/*.json, not this file. */
if (window.I18N) I18N.pack("SKILL",{"chapters":[{"n":0,"title":"When to use","html":"<p>For motion graphics, explainers, ads or music videos in 9:16/16:9 whose scenes are assembled from paper pieces, whose characters are sequence motion or rigs, whose text is kinetic typography in code, and whose render is mgp. Not for full AI video, footage editing (<code>video-making<\/code>), decks (<code>slideshow<\/code>) or vector characters drawn from scratch (<code>vector-character<\/code>).<\/p>\n<p>Related skills (in this package): <code>motion-graphics<\/code> (12 principles, timing, gait, advanced principles; its rule 00 and chapter 0 apply), <code>vector-character<\/code> (vector characters, rigs). Optional skills by the author: <code>papercut-mv<\/code> (older Python-engine MVs; the recipes used here are already copied into the references), <code>transkripsi-lirik<\/code>. The HyperFrames and media-use skills are not needed; if installed, compositions stay compatible.<\/p>\n<p>Priority: the user's instructions, then project rules (FLOW, handover, content rules), then this skill. Conflicting old rules (lo-fi anime taste, Python engine, every job at 1K) are asked about and recorded.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Language.<\/strong> Reply in the user's language. For Indonesian users, write fully in Bahasa Indonesia, and render user-facing templates (T1 brief, gate messages, reports) in Indonesian using the ID template variants (<code>references/template.id.md<\/code>; <code>mgp new --lang id<\/code> writes <code>brief.md<\/code> and <code>STATUS.md<\/code> in Indonesian).<\/p>\n<p><strong>Content rules per project (required).<\/strong> Brief T1 holds the \"Content rules\", and they override every visual choice. Examples: a religious project may forbid depicting certain figures (show them only as light or calligraphy); real people may get no photos or AI images; a brand may forbid showing faces; every quoted source is checked before use.<\/p>"},{"n":1,"title":"Staged workflow and gates","html":"<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage<\/th><th>Work and output<\/th><th>Gate<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>1 Brief<\/td><td>Frame choice (aspect ratio, resolution, fps; 1.1 item 6), then <code>mgp new &lt;folder&gt;<\/code> (standard folders, <code>brief.md<\/code>, <code>STATUS.md<\/code>, <code>project.json<\/code>, <code>index.html<\/code>); <code>mgp doctor<\/code> once at the start; T1, platform duration limits, personification question (2.1)<\/td><td>Answers to the brief form<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>2 Concept + taste<\/td><td>3 T2a concepts; style recommendation + 2-3 T2 options, style frame, 3-5 s test clip; element confirmation<\/td><td><strong>REQUIRED G1<\/strong>: concept, style, elements<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>3 Script<\/td><td>Voice source choice (4 options, 1.1 item 5), script, VO, per-word and per-letter timing, T3 beat sheet; VO recorded in <code>project.json<\/code><\/td><td><strong>REQUIRED G2<\/strong>: voice source + script + VO<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>4 Block plan<\/td><td>Video mental picture, branch per scene, motion library check, image route (Higgsfield or free, from <code>doctor<\/code>), image count, T4 jobs + credits + balance<\/td><td><strong>REQUIRED G3<\/strong>: Higgsfield permission + credit limit (free route: asset list only, no credits)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>5 Generate<\/td><td>Jobs within the G3 permission or <code>mgp image<\/code>, check results, T5 log + <code>project.json<\/code> <code>assets[]<\/code><\/td><td>REPORT<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>6 Cut out<\/td><td>Sprites, meta, anchors, contact sheet<\/td><td>REPORT<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>7 Assemble block 1 (pilot)<\/td><td>(a) <strong>blocking<\/strong>: stepped key poses, <code>mgp contact --var blocking=true --times poses<\/code>; (b) <strong>spline<\/strong>: ease, overlap, springs, then <code>mgp curves<\/code>; T6-T8, audio, preview + block 1 clip, T9a table<\/td><td><strong>REQUIRED G4<\/strong>: (a) blocking approved, then (b) the pilot becomes the look reference for the whole video<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>8 Assemble block 2 on + review<\/td><td>Blocking then spline per block; clip per block + blocking sheet + curves + T9a table, changed ranges only<\/td><td>REPORT<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>9 Render<\/td><td>Delivery + final QA; T10 (<code>STATUS.md<\/code>) every session<\/td><td><strong>REQUIRED G5<\/strong>: the word \"render\" from the user<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>A follow-up episode of a series uses the series style already locked: G1 needs only the concept, personification and changed elements, no new style frame.<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">1.1<\/span> <strong>Two kinds of gate.<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><\/th><th>REQUIRED (G1-G5)<\/th><th>REPORT<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Claude<\/td><td>Sends the options or result, then stops until the user answers<\/td><td>Sends the report (contact sheet, T9a table, clip), then continues in the same turn<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>User<\/td><td>Chooses or approves<\/td><td>May stay silent; may write \"HOLD\" or Review Results at any time<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Late Review Results<\/td><td>-<\/td><td>Only the parts named are redone; other blocks stay<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Condition<\/td><td>Creative decisions, money, final output<\/td><td>Every P0 check for that asset type passes (T9a)<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<ol><li>\"HOLD\" (Indonesian users may write \"TAHAN\") = Claude finishes the running step, starts no new job or render, then waits.<\/li><li>A REPORT gate becomes a stop when a fix needs credits beyond the G3 permission, changes an approved concept/style/script, a P0 check still fails after fixing, or a frame gets worse (chapter 12 item 3).<\/li><li>Every Higgsfield generation outside the G3 list (e.g. a style frame in stage 2) needs permission + a balance check first (4.1). The free route (<code>mgp image<\/code>) needs no credit permission, but is still reported (provider, model, seed, licence).<\/li><li>When the user is away, REQUIRED gates still stop; REPORT gates keep running and the report is left in the chat + T10.<\/li><li><strong>Voice decision point<\/strong> (G2, <code>references/audio.md<\/code> 10.1): before any VO is made, Claude asks with four options, then waits: (a) ElevenLabs (stock voice or the user's own clone), (b) a local voice clone from a ±30 s recording of the reading template (<code>mgp voice template<\/code>), (c) the user's own narration recording, or (d) no narration. GPU/CPU advice comes from <code>mgp doctor<\/code>; the clone engine is installed once per computer with <code>mgp install voice<\/code>. <strong>Ethics:<\/strong> a local clone only with the consent sentence in the recording, or with direct consent from the voice owner, recorded (<code>mgp voice clone ... --manual-consent --statement \"&lt;who, when, how&gt;\"<\/code>). Public figures, celebrities, religious scholars, officials or deceased people are never imitated. Voice profiles stay local; the user's original recording is not copied into the project or package folder.<\/li><li><strong>Frame decision point<\/strong> (stage 1, before <code>mgp new<\/code>; <code>references/brief-concept.md<\/code> 2.0): if the brief names no aspect ratio, Claude asks with options and waits: (a) <strong>9:16<\/strong> 1080x1920 (Reels, TikTok, Shorts, Stories), (b) <strong>16:9<\/strong> 1920x1080 (YouTube, presentations, web), (c) <strong>1:1<\/strong> 1080x1080 (IG/FB/LinkedIn feed), (d) <strong>4:5<\/strong> 1080x1350 (IG/FB feed, largest in the timeline), (e) <strong>21:9<\/strong> 2520x1080 (cinematic, trailer, banner), or (f) <strong>multi-format<\/strong> (one master + other versions; the composition is designed with a shared safe zone from the start, other versions rendered separately). Also ask resolution (1080 default; 720 for drafts; 4K renders ±4x slower) and fps (24 default, 30 or 60). The answer goes into T1 and <code>mgp new --aspect &lt;r&gt; --resolution &lt;p&gt; --fps &lt;n&gt;<\/code>.<\/li><\/ol>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">1.2<\/span> <strong>Working rules.<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol><li>Claude asks before creative decisions or uncertain numbers (chat + interactive question, with options, credit cost, free options); that part waits for the answer.<\/li><li>Claude never invents data: duration from the audio, size from the file, credits from the tool, coordinates and proof texts from the source.<\/li><li>Production runs per block. Block 1 is locked at G4; later blocks are locked when reported as passing T9a, unless the user writes HOLD or Review Results for that block. The block plan holds the timeline and narration text, SFX, the per-second mental picture, asset prompts, code parts and motion over time; a revised VO only needs retiming.<\/li><li>One render at a time; review sends only the changed range. One GPU job at a time (shared GPU lock, <code>references/hardware.md<\/code>).<\/li><li>Documents say \"Review Results\", never \"PM Decision\". Keep chat short, in the user's language, polite and warm.<\/li><li>Estimates: block duration = word count / speaking rate, measured from the first VO. Benchmarks per source: first reference VO v1 2.06 words/s; local clone (test profile) 1.72 words/s, ±1.9 with <code>--speed 1.12<\/code> (<code>references/audio.md<\/code> 10.5). Drawings shown per shot = d x 12; unique drawings are fewer because cycles repeat and the motion library is reused.<\/li><li>Each scene picks one production branch (<code>references/branches.md<\/code>): A direct kit, B reverse stripe, or C AI video. The cheapest in credits comes first; kits and plates may come from the free route (<code>references/image.md<\/code>).<\/li><li><code>project.json<\/code> is updated in the same turn as the file change (VO, scenes, key poses, blocks, captions, assets with their provenance). <code>mgp check<\/code> validates it (<code>references/project.md<\/code>).<\/li><\/ol>\n<h4>Start a project (stage 1)<\/h4>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>mgp new &lt;folder&gt; --aspect 9:16 --fps 24 --duration 30 --title &quot;&lt;title&gt;&quot;   # or --aspect 16:9; --lang id|en\nmgp doctor                                                                # CPU, RAM, GPU/VRAM, encoder, PyTorch\nmgp check &lt;folder&gt;/index.html                                             # contract + project.json<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<ul><li>The <code>index.html<\/code> from <code>mgp new<\/code> already loads mgp.motion (<code>assets/js/motion.js<\/code>; API <code>mgp.motion.*<\/code>, <code>gsap<\/code> is only an alias), <code>assets/js/mgp.js<\/code> (the <code>MGP.*<\/code> library) and the bundled fonts (<code>assets/fonts/fonts.css<\/code>) by relative paths, so it runs from file:// (with a small player) and through mgp. It has a <code>blocking<\/code> variable and a pose-to-pose motion example. <code>brief.md<\/code> and <code>STATUS.md<\/code> follow <code>--lang<\/code> (default: <code>MGP_LANG<\/code>, then <code>LANG<\/code>).<\/li><li>The <code>doctor<\/code> result is used at the decision points (<code>references/hardware.md<\/code>): local voice clone on GPU or CPU, local or free-cloud images, render encoder. If the local engines are not installed (<code>mgp install all --status<\/code>), offer <code>mgp install voice<\/code> / <code>mgp install image<\/code> (once per computer, <code>--detach<\/code> to download in the background; models are not in the package ZIP). Installation runs only after the user agrees (downloads of several GB).<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>Required rules (not negotiable)<\/h4>\n<ol><li><strong>Collage = assembled from pieces.<\/strong> The kit is generated, cut out in code, layered, then animated per piece. One full AI plate + overlays is not collage (rejected). Paper drawn by code is also rejected (childish).<\/li><li><strong>Text only in code<\/strong>, never in AI images; every image prompt forbids text/logos/symbols.<\/li><li><strong>Maps are never drawn by AI<\/strong>: Natural Earth (<code>MGP.map<\/code>) + sourced coordinates, aspect ratio not stretched.<\/li><li><strong>Sequence characters: one shared scale per sheet<\/strong>, soles locked to the baseline; sliding a still drawing is forbidden (foot sliding). Slide speed = 2 x stride length / cycle period.<\/li><li><strong>Split-pin puppet rigs with visible pins are rejected<\/strong>; a moving character = a sequence sheet or a smooth code rig.<\/li><li><strong>One character, one model sheet + identical description<\/strong> in every prompt; the motion library is reused across episodes.<\/li><li><strong>Determinism<\/strong>: no <code>Date.now()<\/code>, unseeded <code>Math.random()<\/code>, fetch, timers or <code>repeat: -1<\/code>; one paused timeline per composition; <code>mgp check<\/code> must give 0 ERROR and be deterministic.<\/li><li><strong>Placeholders are labelled in the frame<\/strong> and named in the delivery; visible weaknesses are stated honestly.<\/li><li><strong>One fix must not break another part<\/strong>: compare frames before/after; if a frame gets worse, stop and report.<\/li><li><strong>The pilot is not splined before blocking is approved<\/strong> (G4 step a).<\/li><li>Full render only after the word \"render\" (G5); review only the changed range.<\/li><li>The full list of rejected approaches and their replacements: <code>references/mistakes.md<\/code> (read before G1 and G4).<\/li><\/ol>"},{"n":2,"title":"Brief, concept, taste, POV","html":"<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">2.0<\/span> <strong>Frame choice<\/strong> (decision point SKILL.md 1.1 item 6). When the brief does not name an aspect ratio yet, Claude asks once, with the options below, then waits:<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option<\/th><th>Size (1080 resolution)<\/th><th>For<\/th><th>Safe zone (text and faces)<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>9:16 vertical<\/td><td>1080 x 1920<\/td><td>Reels, TikTok, Shorts, Stories<\/td><td>Words in the band 168-1330 px of 1920 (top ±9% and bottom ±20-30% covered by UI); avoid the lower right side (buttons); captions at the bottom of the band<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>16:9 horizontal<\/td><td>1920 x 1080<\/td><td>YouTube, presentations, web, TV<\/td><td>Text inside the central 90% (title safe); captions in the lower third<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>1:1 square<\/td><td>1080 x 1080<\/td><td>IG/FB/LinkedIn feed, feed ads<\/td><td>6% margin on every side<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>4:5 portrait<\/td><td>1080 x 1350<\/td><td>IG/FB feed (shows largest in the timeline)<\/td><td>6% margin; the IG grid preview crops to the central 3:4, so keep titles away from the top/bottom edge<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>21:9 cinematic<\/td><td>2520 x 1080<\/td><td>Trailers, banners, openers<\/td><td>Text in the central 80%; the 16:9 version is often cropped from the centre<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Multi-format<\/td><td>One master + derivatives<\/td><td>Cross-platform campaigns<\/td><td>Design inside the safe zone shared by all aspect ratios from the style frame on; important elements in the centre. Each aspect ratio is still assembled and checked on its own (not just cropped), because the text layout differs<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Ask at the same time:<\/p>\n<ul><li><strong>Resolution<\/strong> (short side): 720 for quick drafts, 1080 default, 1440, or 4K (2160; renders ±4x slower, assets must be sharp enough).<\/li><li><strong>fps<\/strong>: 24 default (cinematic feel and stop-motion on twos), 30 (UI, data, social), or 60 (very fast motion, screens).<\/li><\/ul>\n<p>The answers go into T1 rows \"Platform, aspect ratio, duration\" and \"output\", then <code>mgp new &lt;folder&gt; --aspect &lt;r&gt; --resolution &lt;p&gt; --fps &lt;n&gt;<\/code>. For AI assets the job aspect ratio follows the frame (<code>mgp image --aspect<\/code>). A plate is made slightly wider than the frame when the camera will pan.<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">2.1<\/span> <strong>Brief.<\/strong> Claude asks for the empty T1 fields (reference = principle), including personification: used or not, and which object. This question is repeated at the start of every episode or episode plan.<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">2.2<\/span> <strong>Concept<\/strong> (stage 2, before taste). A concept is the device that makes the content visible. Claude sends 3 T2a concepts of at least 2 different types, side by side, with a recommendation + reason, then the pros and cons of each concept.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type<\/th><th>How it works<\/th><th>Story example<\/th><th>Explainer example<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Object metaphor<\/td><td>An abstract idea is represented by an object<\/td><td>Scales with two pans<\/td><td>Puzzle pieces<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Personification<\/td><td>An object becomes a character with a face that acts<\/td><td>Letters with faces wave goodbye one by one as they are sorted into mailbags; one letter is left behind alone<\/td><td>A moon with a face follows the car<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Witness narrator<\/td><td>The story told from an object's point of view<\/td><td>\"I am that last letter\"<\/td><td>\"I am a container, this heavy equipment does not fit\"<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Scale / comparison<\/td><td>Size or time changed so it can be felt<\/td><td>Thousands of letters a day, one left unsent<\/td><td>Earth's age = 24 hours<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Process / cause and effect<\/td><td>A chain of events<\/td><td>Written, sent, sorted, delivered<\/td><td>Dominoes, machines<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Journey / route<\/td><td>Map and a moving line (chapter 9)<\/td><td>The letter's route from town to town<\/td><td>LCT route from the jetty to the site<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Two contrasting worlds<\/td><td>Split screen, before and after<\/td><td>Post office (empty) and village (waiting)<\/td><td>Container ship and LCT<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Data as objects<\/td><td>Numbers become countable piles<\/td><td>Each letter = one house light switched on<\/td><td>One sack = one million tonnes<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<ol><li>One video, one through line; at most 2 types combined.<\/li><li>The meaning-carrying object appears at frame 0 (6.3) and returns in the payoff of the final scene.<\/li><li>The proving motion changes a relationship (2.5).<\/li><li>Personification is for objects only. Figures protected by the content rules are never personified and never become witness narrators. Animation of objects with faces follows 6.7.<\/li><li>The credit estimate in T2a is worked out from the branch table in chapter 15 and the motion library (6.6), and written as an estimate.<\/li><\/ol>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">2.3<\/span> <strong>Taste.<\/strong> Claude recommends 1 style + 2-3 options (T2) with a style frame + clip.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Style family<\/th><th>Traits<\/th><th>Good for<\/th><th>Risk<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Documentary paper collage (default)<\/td><td>Matte paper kit, 7-12 layers, warm light, bright natural colours<\/td><td>History, stories, explainers<\/td><td>Many assets; pieces must be cut clean<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Vox editorial<\/td><td>Halftone, tears, tape, clippings, strong colour field per beat<\/td><td>Arguments, data, opinion<\/td><td>Easily turns into a moving poster<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Golden manuscript<\/td><td>Ivory page, 8-point stars, arched windows, gold lines, DM Serif + gold italic<\/td><td>Scriptural evidence, quotes, chapter titles<\/td><td>Static when longer than 6 s<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Bright paper<\/td><td>Wide chromatic fields, at least 3 hues, warm accent 3-15%<\/td><td>Cheerful education, products<\/td><td>Childish without real paper texture<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Elements are confirmed one by one (T2 list), not guessed; a test clip is made before mass production.<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">2.4<\/span> <strong>POV<\/strong> is chosen per shot in T3:<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>POV<\/th><th>When; rules<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Observer<\/td><td>Opening a location, general action; eye level, the same horizon at the same location<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Character / from behind<\/td><td>Following a journey, a faceless character; moving away = rising and getting smaller<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Top-down<\/td><td>Maps, routes, distances; real map data (chapter 9)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Insert / macro<\/td><td>Small objects, hands, scripture cards; background = the same set, blurred<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Directions are written in frame terms (\"frame left/right\"), not the character's left and right; every location has a \"Screen directions\" row, and the 180-degree axis is kept.<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">2.5<\/span> One beat holds one idea and one action; a beat with no new graphic need reuses the same set. The main motion proves the claim by changing a relationship (flowing, narrowing, piling up, replacing).<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">2.6<\/span> Layout archetypes: asymmetric stage, diagonal cascade, split contrast, radial cycle, depth corridor, pile of consequences, map route, full-screen data. Adjacent scenes differ in archetype or dominant colour; 4 or more scenes use at least 3 archetypes.<\/p>"},{"n":3,"title":"Collage visual system","html":"<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">3.0<\/span> <strong>Atelier Kertas (house style).<\/strong> The name of this package's default style; a brief that says \"house style\", \"Atelier Kertas\", or names no style uses it. It consists of:<\/p>\n<ul><li><strong>Material:<\/strong> every element is a real piece of matte cardstock/paper: clean scissor-cut edges, fine paper fibre, card thickness visible at the edge, shading only inside the piece, shot straight from the front, clean daylight, rich local colours. Not cartoon, anime, 3D render, glossy, sepia, or a vintage filter. The official prompt text = <code>--style paper<\/code> in <code>mgp image<\/code> (<code>render/src/image.mjs<\/code>, <code>STYLES.paper<\/code>) and the Higgsfield STYLE block (<code>references/image.md<\/code> 4.3).<\/li><li><strong>Assembly:<\/strong> a scene is assembled from separate pieces (3.1), at least 3 planes + a character with parallax (3.2); sky, light, masks, text, and maps from code; one cast-shadow direction; contact shadows; multiply grain 0.10; thin cream rim; torn edges only on text frames and transitions.<\/li><li><strong>Motion:<\/strong> pieces and characters on twos with a small boil (6.1); element entrances with a paper pop/settle (6.2); sequence sheet characters or a smooth rig (no visible split-pins); paper transitions (tear, fold, page-turn; chapter 8).<\/li><li><strong>Text:<\/strong> only in code, bundled fonts, paper cards for headlines (chapter 7).<\/li><li><strong>Variants:<\/strong> <code>--style vector|origami|pixel<\/code> (17.3) are other styles, not Atelier Kertas. A series that has already locked its style uses its earlier kit/references as the style anchor, which is stronger than prompt words.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">3.1<\/span> Paper collage is a scene ASSEMBLED from separate paper pieces: the kit is generated (chapter 4), cut out in code (chapter 5), layered, then animated piece by piece (chapter 6). One full AI plate with an overlay is NOT a collage (rejected at review). A 9:16 plate is only for a style anchor or an interior, and is better split into a kit or into layers (chapter 15, branch B).<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">3.2<\/span> Higgsfield paper supplies objects, sets, characters, the sun, light discs, and torn strips. Code supplies only the sky gradient, light, masks, typography, maps, and calligraphy.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Element<\/th><th>Rules and numbers (720 w)<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Planes<\/td><td>At least 3 planes + a character (reference reels 7-12 layers). Parallax k: sky/clouds 0.12-0.18; far hills 0.25; near hills 0.40; buildings 0.50-0.62; hero 0.70-0.74; foreground 0.88-0.92; character 0.95-1.0; framing elements 1.1-1.3<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Hero<\/td><td>One focus with about 70% of the visual weight; at most 2-3 supporting elements<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Sky<\/td><td>Code gradient, day #2C67B0, #78ACDC (55%), #D9E8F2 at y 780<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Cast shadow<\/td><td>One direction. Clouds dy 4 px, blur 6 px, rgba(25,55,90,.22); objects dy 5-6 px, blur 9-11 px, rgba(45,28,12,.30-.34)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Contact shadow<\/td><td>Ellipse 0.36-0.42 x figure width, height 10-14 px, blur 6-8 px, opacity 0.30<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Light<\/td><td>Radial rgba(255,240,205,.18) from (120, 80), radius 900 px<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Grain<\/td><td>Multiply over the whole frame, fixed seed, 0.10-0.16 (default 0.10)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Boil<\/td><td>Pieces ±0.7 px per drawing; characters ±1-2 px, ±0.4-0.5°<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Edges<\/td><td>Clean cut in the scenery; torn only on text frames and transitions; cream rim 0.5-1% of the width<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Palette<\/td><td>Local colour per object. Dominant field 35-60%, supporting 20-40%, neutral 5-25%, accent 3-15%, brown/grey at most 20%, at least 3 hues<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">3.3<\/span> Scene logic:<\/p>\n<ul><li>Props have context (attached, sat on, water in a dam) and a scale that fits the character's body; props with no purpose are removed. A sitting pose has a seat, a lap line, and a contact shadow (recipe from the papercut-mv method 5.2: the lap line is measured per sheet as a fraction of the sprite height from the sole baseline; the seat prop is scaled so its feet are at the soles and its seat line meets the lap line; the character sits slightly deeper, ±2.5% of the sprite height; contact shadows on the seat and under the soles; a full-res crop of every sitting scene is checked).<\/li><li>The hero stands apart from the background at thumbnail size through hue and lightness.<\/li><li>Geography, sun direction, and character size stay the same across shots; layers are widened so their edges are never visible.<\/li><li>Light medallion for holy figures: a layered disc and rays from the kit, a code screen glow (radius about 2.1 R), calligraphy (7.4).<\/li><\/ul>"},{"n":4,"title":"Image assets: free (`mgp image`) and Higgsfield","html":"<p>Read by Claude when a project needs a <strong>kit sheet<\/strong> (character/object pieces for a rig) or a <strong>plate<\/strong> (background) and the user chooses the free route, or there is no Higgsfield balance. Motion is still made in code (rig, walk cycle, folding, lip-sync). Per-frame AI sequences (a different pose in every frame) stay on the Higgsfield route, because consistency across frames needs a large model with strong references; the free route here is only used for one sheet per character/location.<\/p>\n<p>Checked 05-11-2026. Prices, quotas and terms of service can change; check the source links again when in doubt.<\/p>\n<h4>A. Free route: <code>mgp image<\/code> (v2.4)<\/h4>\n<h4>A.1. Available routes<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Route (<code>--provider<\/code>)<\/th><th>Model<\/th><th>Model licence / output terms<\/th><th>What is free<\/th><th>Account / key<\/th><th>Reference<\/th><th>Size<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td><code>hf<\/code>: official BFL Space (ZeroGPU)<\/td><td>FLUX.2 [klein] 4B (default), FLUX.1 [schnell]<\/td><td>Apache-2.0, output may be used commercially<\/td><td>Daily ZeroGPU GPU quota. Without an account ±4-5 images, then it runs out (tested); a free account + <code>HF_TOKEN<\/code> gets a larger quota<\/td><td>Not required; <code>HF_TOKEN<\/code> optional<\/td><td>klein: yes (max 4)<\/td><td>klein max 1024 per side, schnell 2048<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>cloudflare<\/code>: Workers AI<\/td><td><code>@cf/black-forest-labs/flux-2-klein-4b<\/code> (default), <code>flux-1-schnell<\/code><\/td><td>Apache-2.0 + BFL terms: the output belongs to the user, may not be used to train other models, may not be claimed as made by a human<\/td><td>10,000 neurons/day (reset 00:00 UTC). klein 1024x1024 = 4 tiles x 26.05 = ±104 neurons (±95 images/day; every reference tile +5.37); schnell 4 tiles x 4.8 + 4 steps x 9.6 = ±58 neurons (±170/day)<\/td><td>Free account; <code>CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID<\/code> + <code>CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN<\/code><\/td><td>klein: yes (<code>input_image_0..3<\/code>, preferably &lt;= 512 px)<\/td><td>klein 256-1920; schnell fixed 1024x1024<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>pollinations<\/code><\/td><td><code>black-forest-labs/flux.2-klein-4b<\/code><\/td><td>Model licence (Apache-2.0); Pollinations terms: \"Model licences vary; verify before commercial use\"<\/td><td>Free Pollen quests for non-paid models (amount varies, not promised)<\/td><td>GitHub login; key <code>sk_...<\/code> in <code>POLLINATIONS_KEY<\/code><\/td><td>klein: yes<\/td><td>max 2048<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>local<\/code>: <code>py/image_engine.py<\/code><\/td><td>FLUX.2 [klein] 4B GGUF Q4_K_M + Qwen3-4B GGUF (default); FLUX.1 [schnell] GGUF Q4_K_S + T5-XXL GGUF<\/td><td>Apache-2.0<\/td><td>Free forever, no internet after the download<\/td><td>None<\/td><td>klein: yes<\/td><td>any (multiples of 16)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>higgsfield<\/code> (<code>--source higgsfield<\/code>)<\/td><td>GPT Image 2.5 (paid)<\/td><td>Higgsfield terms<\/td><td>Paid<\/td><td>Higgsfield connector in Claude<\/td><td>Yes<\/td><td>1K/2K<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Rejected (not used at all):<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Candidate<\/th><th>Reason<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Gemini API (Gemini 2.5 Flash Image, 3.1 Flash Image, 3 Pro Image / \"Nano Banana\")<\/td><td>Official pricing page: the Free Tier column for every image output model says \"Not available\". No free route<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Hugging Face Inference Providers (router, <code>hf-inference<\/code>)<\/td><td>Free account: \"Monthly Credits: None\", credits must be bought. The ZeroGPU Space (<code>hf<\/code>) is used instead<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>SDXL-Turbo<\/td><td>Licence <code>sai-nc-community<\/code> (non-commercial; commercial use needs a Stability membership)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>FLUX.1 Kontext [dev], FLUX.2 [klein] 9B<\/td><td>Non-commercial FLUX licence<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Pollinations without a key (<code>image.pollinations.ai/prompt/...<\/code>)<\/td><td>Still works, but only the Sana 512 px model with a logo watermark that cannot be switched off; quality far below what a kit needs. The new endpoint <code>gen.pollinations.ai<\/code> refuses without a key (401)<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<h4>A.2. Choosing a route (rules for Claude)<\/h4>\n<ol><li>Run <code>mgp image providers<\/code> (which keys are already set) and <code>mgp doctor<\/code> (VRAM, free RAM, torch CUDA).<\/li><li><strong>Character consistency needed<\/strong> (a second kit for the same character, a face kit, other poses): use the klein model with <code>--reference<\/code> (model sheet or the first kit). schnell does not accept references.<\/li><li>Order of <code>--source auto<\/code> (default): <code>cloudflare<\/code> when the key is set, then <code>hf<\/code> (no key), then <code>pollinations<\/code> when the key is set, then <code>local<\/code> when the weights are already downloaded. A quota failure moves on to the next automatically; <code>auto<\/code> never silently triggers a large model download.<\/li><li><strong>Local<\/strong> is chosen when: there is no internet / the cloud quota has run out / many variations are needed. Requirements: the local engine is installed (<code>mgp install image<\/code>, weights ±4.9 GB; section 4), free RAM &gt;= 11 GB (text encoder phase), and preferably an NVIDIA GPU &gt;= 4 GB. Without a GPU it runs on the CPU but slowly (see 5).<\/li><li>Per-frame AI sequences, 2-state crowds, branch B keyframes, AI video: <strong>stay on Higgsfield<\/strong> (Part B chapter 4). The free route is not used for these. <code>--source higgsfield<\/code> only prints a guide + a ready-to-paste prompt; Claude then uses the Higgsfield connector with gate G3 (balance, job list, the user's OK).<\/li><li>Every result is checked like a Higgsfield asset (Part B 4.4 item 5, and <code>references/cutout.md<\/code> 5.4): number of parts, nothing touching, same costume, flat background, no text/logos. Failure: change <code>--seed<\/code>, do not patch.<\/li><li>Credits in T5: write the provider, model, seed, size (in <code>&lt;output&gt;.json<\/code> next to the PNG). Cost 0. Also record it in <code>project.json<\/code> <code>assets[]<\/code> (<code>source: \"mgp-image\"<\/code>, <code>provider<\/code>, <code>model<\/code>, <code>seed<\/code>, <code>license<\/code>, <code>meta<\/code>).<\/li><\/ol>\n<p>How to get free keys (also printed by <code>mgp image providers<\/code>):<\/p>\n<ul><li><strong>Cloudflare<\/strong>: sign up at &lt;https://dash.cloudflare.com/sign-up&gt; (Free account; the Workers AI documentation does not mention a credit card for the free allocation), copy the Account ID, create a token at &lt;https://dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens&gt; with the \"Workers AI\" template, then <code>setx CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID ...<\/code> and <code>setx CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN ...<\/code>.<\/li><li><strong>Hugging Face<\/strong>: &lt;https://huggingface.co/join&gt;, a \"Read\" token at &lt;https://huggingface.co/settings/tokens&gt;, <code>setx HF_TOKEN hf_...<\/code>.<\/li><li><strong>Pollinations<\/strong>: GitHub login at &lt;https://enter.pollinations.ai&gt;, create an <code>sk_<\/code> key on the keys page, <code>setx POLLINATIONS_KEY sk_...<\/code>.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>A.3. Recipes<\/h4>\n<h5>A.3.1 Character kit for a rig (default: template method)<\/h5>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>mgp image kit &quot;a ten year old Indonesian boy, short black hair, warm brown skin, mustard yellow t-shirt, navy shorts, white sneakers&quot; \\\n  --style paper --seed 11 -o assets/kit_budi\n# consistency with a model sheet that is already approved:\nmgp image kit &quot;&lt;identical description&gt;&quot; --reference assets/model_budi.png -o assets/kit_budi2<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>How it works:<\/p>\n<ol><li><code>image_engine.py template<\/code> makes a <strong>slot sheet<\/strong>: plain grey shapes for 8 parts (head, torso, upper_arm, lower_arm, hand / thigh, shin, foot) on a magenta background, widely spaced, one scale.<\/li><li>The slot sheet is sent as reference 1 (klein multi-reference edit). The prompt asks for every grey shape to be replaced by the matching body part, background kept #FF00FF. Extra references (model sheet) become reference 2.<\/li><li>Magenta key (the logic of <code>scripts/magenta_key.py<\/code>), then <strong>slice<\/strong>: connected components, names from the slots (the component that covers most of the slot box), one PNG per part.<\/li><li><code>kit.json<\/code>: <code>parts[]<\/code> with <code>name<\/code>, <code>file<\/code>, box on the sheet (<code>x, y, w, h<\/code>), <code>pivot<\/code> (rotation point, top end of the part; head: bottom end/neck) and <code>tip<\/code> (child joint), in pixels of the part PNG. <code>_preview.png<\/code>: boxes + names + pivot (red) + tip (green) on a dark blue background for QC.<\/li><li>One part per type: left and right limbs use the same PNG; the one at the back is darkened and placed behind the torso (classic cut-out trick). Small models follow 8 slots better than 14.<\/li><\/ol>\n<p>Automatic QC in slice (shown as <code>warning<\/code> in <code>kit.json<\/code> and in the log): empty template slot (part touching or missing), part count does not match, and leftover template grey &gt; 15% of a part's pixels (the slot shape was not replaced; a character in grey clothes can trigger a false warning). Test result: of 3 seeds, 1 clean; so use <code>--tries 3<\/code> (consecutive seeds until the slice is clean, the result of each seed in <code>s&lt;seed&gt;/<\/code>). Small corrections without regenerating: <code>mgp image slice sheet_cutout.png --template template.json --rotate hand:180 -o kit/<\/code> (e.g. a hand drawn with the fingers pointing up).<\/p>\n<p>Without a template (<code>--method prompt<\/code>, or schnell): a CANVAS/CHARACTER/PIECES/STYLE/ONLY prompt in the T4 style. The 4B model often makes a <strong>full character turnaround<\/strong> instead of separate parts (tested); such a result is still useful as a model sheet for <code>--reference<\/code>, not as a kit.<\/p>\n<p>Slice any sheet again (e.g. a Higgsfield kit): <code>mgp image slice sheet_cutout.png -o kit/ --names head,torso,...<\/code> (names in reading order: row band, then x; with the 8 standard names, names are guessed from the shape) or <code>--template template.json<\/code>.<\/p>\n<h5>A.3.2 Plate<\/h5>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>mgp image plate &quot;a quiet kampung street at morning, warm light from frame left, low fences and potted plants&quot; --aspect 16:9 -o assets/plate.png<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The prompt follows T4 (b): camera, vanishing point, \"Empty scene: no people\", lower third empty.<\/p>\n<p>Note on the local route: plates from local FLUX.2 klein (and the free cloud) tend to be <strong>semi-photographic, not collage<\/strong> (section 5; 30 s ad test: the table plate came out semi-photographic). Such a plate is good enough as a far background with grain and a little blur; near planes and objects are still made as separate magenta assets (3.3), then assembled. For a full collage plate: the Higgsfield route (Part B) or split the plate into layers (branch B).<\/p>\n<h5>A.3.3 One asset on a magenta background<\/h5>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>mgp image &quot;a carved wooden gate with lattice windows&quot; --magenta --cutout --seed 3 -o assets/gate.png\n# -&gt; gate.png (raw) + gate_cutout.png (alpha) + gate.json (meta)<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p><code>--magenta<\/code> adds the 17.4 recipe: flat #FF00FF background without shadows, every opening magenta, the whole object with a wide margin, no magenta/pink/purple on the object, no text.<\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>mgp image &quot;a red paper coffee cup&quot; --style paper --magenta --cutout --tries 3 --seed 5 -o assets/cup.png<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<ul><li><code>--style &lt;name|text&gt;<\/code> adds a <code>STYLE:<\/code> block to a free prompt (ignored with a message when the prompt already contains <code>STYLE:<\/code>). Without <code>--style<\/code>, a free prompt is sent as it is.<\/li><li><code>--tries N<\/code> (needs <code>--cutout<\/code>) tries seeds <code>s, s+1, ...<\/code> until the cutout is <strong>clean<\/strong>: no magenta left, the subject does not touch the image edge, magenta tint on the subject &lt;= 2%, subject area 1-95% (<code>py/image_engine.py score-cutout<\/code>). It stops at the first clean one; when there is none, the best-scoring one is used. The best is copied to <code>-o<\/code> (+ <code>_cutout.png<\/code> + <code>.json<\/code> with the score of every try); all tries stay as <code>&lt;name&gt;_s&lt;seed&gt;.png<\/code>. Without <code>--cutout<\/code>, <code>--tries<\/code> is refused with a message (there is nothing to score).<\/li><li>Calibration of score-cutout on 6 test ad assets: 5 clean; a laptop \"slightly purple from magenta reflection\" was flagged (tint 5.4%). A dark red drop shadow under the object is not detected; still check the crop (3.6 item 6).<\/li><\/ul>\n<h5>A.3.4 Style<\/h5>\n<p><code>--style paper<\/code> (default for <code>kit<\/code> and <code>plate<\/code>; for free prompts added only when written; the STYLE block 4.3, flat-background version: shadows only inside the piece = house style Atelier Kertas, <code>references/style.md<\/code> 3.0), <code>vector<\/code>, <code>origami<\/code>, <code>pixel<\/code>, or free text. For a paper style that is already approved, a style reference (an old kit) is stronger than words.<\/p>\n<h5>A.3.5 Animation from a kit<\/h5>\n<p><code>render/examples/kit-rig.html<\/code> assembles <code>kit.json<\/code> into a DOM joint rig (hip → thigh → shin → foot; neck → head; shoulder → upper arm → lower arm → hand) and runs a walk cycle over a sliding plate background.<\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>mgp contact kit-rig.html --n 8 -o contact.png\nmgp render kit-rig.html -o walk.mp4<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>For production, replace that simple cycle with the motion library <code>motion-graphics/lib<\/code> (gait, springs) or the <code>vector-character<\/code> rig; the pivots from <code>kit.json<\/code> become <code>transform-origin<\/code> directly.<\/p>\n<h5>A.3.6 One pose per image for paper stop-motion (paper-style anime character test, 05-11-2026)<\/h5>\n<p>Test material: a paper-style anime character, a 4-pose walk, local FLUX.2 klein 4B with <code>--reference<\/code>.<\/p>\n<ol><li><strong>One pose per image.<\/strong> One sheet with many poses FAILED: every figure stood upright and the paper texture was lost. Ask for one figure, one pose, magenta background (<code>--magenta --cutout<\/code>).<\/li><li><strong>Standing reference + a detailed limb description.<\/strong> With <code>--reference<\/code> the character stays consistent (face, hair, costume), but the model tends to <strong>copy the reference pose<\/strong>. For other poses, use a STANDING reference of the character and describe the limbs one by one, for example \"left leg forward, knee slightly bent, heel touching the ground; right leg behind, toes pushing off; left arm swinging back, right arm forward; body leaning slightly forward, facing frame right\".<\/li><li><strong>Expect ±50% success<\/strong> for difficult poses (2 of the 4 test poses). Use <code>--tries N<\/code> (together with <code>--cutout<\/code>; 3.3: it picks the cleanest cutout, not the correct pose) or other seeds, then pick the pose from a contact sheet; do not patch a wrong pose.<\/li><li><strong>2-4 poses are enough for paper stop-motion on fours<\/strong> (6 images/s: each pose held 4 frames at 24 fps). One shared scale and the soles on one baseline (<code>references/cutout.md<\/code> 5.3), not a scale per pose.<\/li><li><strong>Smoother motion = cut a kit, then rig it.<\/strong> When 2-4 poses feel choppy, make a kit (3.1) of the same character and move it with a code rig (3.5, <code>motion-graphics/lib<\/code>), instead of adding AI poses.<\/li><li><strong>Magenta left after <code>--cutout<\/code> is cleaned automatically<\/strong> (default in <code>mgp image --cutout<\/code>, <code>kit<\/code>, and <code>slice<\/code>; switch off with <code>--no-clean<\/code>). What the cleaner does (<code>render/py/cutout.py<\/code>, <code>clean()<\/code>, numpy + Pillow):<ul><li>leftover magenta = <code>min(R, B) - G &gt; 40<\/code> and <code>R &gt; 120<\/code>; those pixels and alpha &lt; 128 become transparent, the rest 255;<\/li><li>8-neighbour connected components (scipy when available, otherwise a per-row union-find; same result): keep components &gt;= 2% of the area of the largest component. Kit sheets (<code>kit<\/code>, <code>slice<\/code>) do NOT drop small components, because hands/feet are valid;<\/li><li>dropped pixels get the average colour of the kept neighbours, so blur does not bring up a pink fringe;<\/li><li>alpha gets a 0.6 px Gaussian blur. Cropping to the alpha &gt; 40 box happens during assembly, not in the cleaner.<\/li><li>Test on 3 anime poses: leftover magenta 11,288 / 47 / 18 px became 0; a loose 31 px speck was dropped; &lt;0.6 s per image.<\/li><li>Limits: magenta/bright purple clothes or objects are erased too (use <code>--no-clean<\/code>); a dark red drop shadow drawn by the model is not treated as magenta and stays.<\/li><li>full key with despill for magenta backgrounds: <code>scripts/magenta_key.py<\/code> (17.4, Part B).<\/li><li><strong>Despill (default since 06-11-2026; P2_iklan_105: cup \"magenta tint 3.76 %\" was only reported).<\/strong> After the cleaner, <code>--cutout<\/code>, <code>kit<\/code>, <code>slice<\/code> and <code>mgp cutout --key magenta<\/code> take the key-colour excess out of the subject along the alpha edge (<code>cutout.py<\/code>, <code>despill()<\/code>): magenta <code>min(R,B) - G<\/code> is subtracted from R and B (green: <code>G - max(R,B)<\/code> from G; any <code>#rrggbb<\/code> through its dominant channels), full strength within 1.5 % of the short side (15 px at 1024) of a transparent pixel and half up to twice that, the lost brightness spread back over R, G, B (no dark rim); interior colours stay. Measured: cup 3.76 % → 0.28 % (now clean, score 0), anime poses 0.6-1.4 % → &lt;= 0.06 %; skin, yellow and navy unchanged. Switch off: <code>--no-despill<\/code>. A cutout made earlier: <code>python -I render/py/cutout.py despill in.png out.png --key-color magenta [--despill-radius 20 | --everywhere]<\/code> (<code>--everywhere<\/code> = whole subject, for footage lit by a coloured screen). A model cutout (rembg) of a subject shot on green: <code>mgp cutout in.png --despill green<\/code>.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><li>Long per-frame AI sequences (a different pose in every frame) stay on the Higgsfield route (section 2 item 5).<\/li><\/ol>\n<h4>A.4. Local: install and memory<\/h4>\n<p>Install once per computer (Claude offers it at the start of a project from the <code>mgp doctor<\/code> results; it may run in the background while the brief is being worked on):<\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>node motion-graphics-pro/render/bin/mgp.mjs install image --detach     # or: install all (voice + image)\nnode motion-graphics-pro/render/bin/mgp.mjs install image --status       # progress / status of each part<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<ul><li>Venv <code>~/.mgp/venv-image<\/code> (short path) with the locked packages of <code>render/py/requirements-image.txt<\/code> (diffusers 0.42.1, transformers 5.22.1, accelerate, gguf, huggingface_hub), installed with <code>--no-deps<\/code>. The voice venv uses diffusers 0.29 + transformers 5.2, locked by chatterbox-tts, so the two <strong>cannot share one venv<\/strong>.<\/li><li>PyTorch is <strong>not installed twice<\/strong>: when the voice venv exists (same Python minor version, torch 2.6.0), the image venv gets a <code>zz_mgp_torch.pth<\/code> file (an old <code>zz_rekam_torch.pth<\/code> is still used) that points to the voice venv's site-packages; the image venv's own packages still come first. pip runs while the <code>.pth<\/code> is switched off, so it never touches the voice venv's packages. Without a voice venv, torch is installed on its own (the build is chosen the same way as for voice: cu126/cu118/MPS/ROCm/CPU).<\/li><li>FLUX.2 [klein] 4B GGUF Q4_K_M + Qwen3-4B GGUF Q4_K_M + VAE/config weights (17 files, 4.92 GB) are locked to a commit + sha256 in <code>render/py/model-manifest.json<\/code>, downloaded to <code>~/.mgp/model/hf<\/code> (Hugging Face cache layout) with resume.<\/li><li>Local images are not advised when total RAM &lt; 16 GB (needs <code>--force<\/code>); without a GPU it is very slow.<\/li><li>Smoke test: one 512x512 image through the shared GPU lock. When free RAM &lt; 11 GB, the test is marked PENDING (the install is still valid); repeat it later: <code>mgp install image --components smoke-test<\/code>.<\/li><li>An old manual install (e.g. venv <code>~/vgb<\/code> + <code>~/vgb-model/hf<\/code>, or env <code>MGP_IMAGE_PYTHON<\/code> / <code>MGP_IMAGE_MODEL<\/code>) is recognised by <code>--status<\/code>; <code>mgp install image --adopt<\/code> registers it after the sha256 matches, without downloading again.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p><strong>Manual way<\/strong> (without <code>mgp install<\/code>): create a venv on a short path, <code>pip install --no-deps -r render/py/requirements-image.txt<\/code>, borrow torch with a <code>.pth<\/code> file containing the path of the voice venv's site-packages (add it AFTER pip), then <code>mgp image download<\/code> (plain resumable HTTP; xet is switched off because it stalls on a ±1 MB/s network) and point to it with <code>MGP_IMAGE_PYTHON<\/code> + <code>MGP_IMAGE_MODEL<\/code>. Full steps: <code>render/README.md<\/code>, section <code>mgp image<\/code>.<\/p>\n<ul><li>Memory-saving order: the Qwen3-4B text encoder (bf16, measured peak RSS 11 GB, ±100 s) runs on the CPU and is then dropped; only then are the GGUF transformer (2.6 GB) + VAE loaded onto the GPU through the shared GPU lock (<code>py/device.py<\/code>, task <code>flux2-klein-4b-gguf<\/code> in <code>hardware-requirements.json<\/code>). When VRAM is short: <code>enable_model_cpu_offload<\/code>; when CUDA runs out of memory: retry on the CPU.<\/li><li>Free RAM &lt; 11 GB: the command stops with a message (it is not forced). <code>MGP_IMAGE_FORCE=1<\/code> forces it.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p>Measured numbers on an RTX 500 Ada 4 GB laptop: see section 5 below and <code>render/README.md<\/code>, section <code>mgp image<\/code>.<\/p>\n<h4>A.5. Test results (05-11-2026, RTX 500 Ada 4 GB, Core Ultra 7 165H)<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Test<\/th><th>Result<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td><code>hf<\/code> Space klein, free prompt 768x512<\/td><td>10 s; quality equal to local<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>hf<\/code> Space anonymous quota<\/td><td>used up after ±5 requests (including 2 refused for &gt; 1024 px); <code>event: error<\/code> without a message; not recovered after ±1 hour<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Local klein 768x512 without reference<\/td><td>diffusion 9 s, peak VRAM 3.47 GB; total 112 s (text encoder 57 s load + 33 s encode)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Local plate 1344x752<\/td><td>diffusion 22 s, VRAM 3.78 GB<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Local kit 1024x576 + template + 1 character reference (ref 1024 px)<\/td><td>diffusion 158-218 s (VRAM 4.31 GB: spilled into shared memory), 1 of 2 seeds clean<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Local kit, reference scaled down to 640 px (default)<\/td><td>diffusion 76 s<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Kit without template (<code>--method prompt<\/code>)<\/td><td>the model makes a 4-view turnaround, not separate parts<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Location kit of 8 objects (T4 a style)<\/td><td>6 components: some objects stuck together (hill+house+tree); Higgsfield separated all 8<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Quality compared with a Higgsfield kit (<code>guide/assets/img/kit-sheet.jpg<\/code>): the paper fibre texture is much thinner (more like flat cardboard/foam board), colours are more saturated, pieces sometimes stick together; plates tend to be semi-photographic, not collage. The magenta key is clean (no pink fringe in the preview), but a drop shadow that the model still draws leaves a thin dark red line on the lower side of the piece; check it on the contact sheet.<\/p>\n<h4>A.6. Sources<\/h4>\n<ul><li>FLUX.2 [klein] 4B: &lt;https://huggingface.co/black-forest-labs/FLUX.2-klein-4B&gt; (Apache-2.0, \"Open weights available for commercial use\")<\/li><li>FLUX.1 [schnell]: &lt;https://huggingface.co/black-forest-labs/FLUX.1-schnell&gt; (Apache-2.0; gated repo, mirror &lt;https://huggingface.co/unsloth/FLUX.1-schnell&gt;)<\/li><li>FLUX.2 [klein] 9B (flux-non-commercial-license), FLUX.1 Kontext [dev] (flux-1-dev-non-commercial-license): model cards on Hugging Face<\/li><li>SDXL-Turbo: &lt;https://huggingface.co/stabilityai/sdxl-turbo&gt; (sai-nc-community)<\/li><li>Cloudflare pricing: &lt;https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers-ai/platform/pricing/&gt;; klein: &lt;https://developers.cloudflare.com/changelog/post/2026-01-15-flux-2-klein-4b-workers-ai/&gt;; schnell: &lt;https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers-ai/models/flux-1-schnell/&gt;<\/li><li>BFL terms: &lt;https://bfl.ai/legal/terms-of-service&gt;<\/li><li>HF Inference Providers pricing: &lt;https://huggingface.co/docs/inference-providers/pricing&gt;<\/li><li>klein Space: &lt;https://huggingface.co/spaces/black-forest-labs/FLUX.2-klein-4B&gt; (API <code>/gradio_api/info<\/code>)<\/li><li>Gemini pricing: &lt;https://aistudio.google.com/docs/pricing&gt;<\/li><li>Pollinations API: &lt;https://github.com/pollinations/pollinations/blob/main/APIDOCS.md&gt;; model list <code>https://gen.pollinations.ai/image/models<\/code>; terms: &lt;https://github.com/pollinations/pollinations/blob/main/pollinations.ai/public/legal/TERMS_OF_SERVICE.md&gt;<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>B. Higgsfield assets<\/h4>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">4.1<\/span> <strong>Gate.<\/strong> Before generating, Claude asks the user to confirm Higgsfield access, checks the balance (tool <code>balance<\/code>), writes the job list + credit estimate, then waits for an OK. Job IDs, credits and balance are recorded in T5. This is the REQUIRED gate G3 (chapter 1.1): the approval covers the written job list and credit limit; failed jobs (4.4 item 5) are redone within that limit and reported; new jobs or credits above the limit need a new approval.<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">4.2<\/span> <strong>Settings.<\/strong> GPT Image 2.5 (<code>gpt_image_2_5<\/code>), quality high. Tests at 1K (1.5 credits per image); production kits and sequence sheets at 2K (2.75 credits). 21:9: 1K 1344 x 576, 2K 2688 x 1152. 9:16: 1K 752 x 1344, 2K 1520 x 2688. At most 4 parallel jobs. Error 429 = a failed submission with no credits charged; resend only that job. Local uploads are blocked by the proxy; references are taken from existing Higgsfield jobs or media.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type<\/th><th>Aspect ratio, res<\/th><th>Contents<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Kit set<\/td><td>21:9 2K<\/td><td>6-12 pieces of one location, one scale, spaced apart; plain white background (white pieces: plain mid blue)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Sequence sheet<\/td><td>21:9 2K<\/td><td>6 frames of one action, same size, soles on one baseline, white gutters<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Face kit<\/td><td>21:9 1K-2K<\/td><td>8 mouths + 6 pairs of eyebrows (+ 6 pairs of eyes for objects with faces)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>2-state crowd<\/td><td>21:9 2K<\/td><td>Every person calm + reacting, side by side<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Model sheet / plate<\/td><td>21:9 / 9:16 2K<\/td><td>4 views + 3 character close-ups (6.6; a 5-view turnaround when needed) / style anchor, interior<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Keyframe + layer-split sheet<\/td><td>9:16 2K + 21:9 2K<\/td><td>Branch B (chapter 15)<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">4.3<\/span> <strong>STYLE block<\/strong> (proven; pasted identically into every prompt):<\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>Every element is a separate piece of real matte cardstock or textured paper with clean scissor-cut edges, stacked in visible depth layers with soft natural drop shadows between layers, subtle paper fibre grain, slight card thickness visible on edges, photographed straight on. Bright clean daylight, clean whites, rich natural colours. Not cartoon, not anime, not 3D render, not glossy, no sepia wash, no vintage filter. No readable text, letters, numbers, symbols, signs, logos or watermarks anywhere.<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">4.4<\/span> <strong>Prompt rules.<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol><li>Prompts in English: CANVAS, PIECES (or CHARACTER + FRAMES), STYLE, ONLY. Forbidden objects are not named outside STYLE; prompts do not ask for numbers or labels.<\/li><li>The character description is identical in every prompt; background characters are written out in full (age, clothes, head covering, facing direction); directions use frame terms. A plate names the camera, vanishing point, \"Empty scene: no people\", and the empty area.<\/li><li>Old job IDs become image_references (style anchor for kits; the character's model sheet for every sequence, crowd and face kit of that character (6.6); the parent kit for derivatives).<\/li><li>Objects with writing are described as plain (\"plain with simple shapes, no printing\"); figures forbidden by the content rules are never requested.<\/li><li>Every result is checked (count, nothing touching, same costume, flat background, no text/logos/wrong symbols); failed jobs are redone without asking.<\/li><\/ol>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">4.5<\/span> <strong>AI video.<\/strong> Image-to-video only for 2-3 hero shots that cannot be achieved with pieces (smoke, water, subtle acting), never for sacred figures. Start frame = the approved still; the prompt follows the skill's video prompt rules (shot type, camera and lens, location, layout map, timed shots, people references, one location per generation).<\/p>"},{"n":5,"title":"Cutting and keying","html":"<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">5.1<\/span> <strong>Kit cutter<\/strong> (algorithm of <code>cut_kit_v5.py<\/code> from the papercut-mv method; that script is not shipped in this pack). In this pack the steps are run by <code>mgp image slice<\/code> (alpha sheet → one PNG per part + <code>kit.json<\/code> + preview; <code>--magenta<\/code> keys the magenta background first, <code>--min-area<\/code>, <code>--names<\/code>) and <code>mgp cutout --key magenta<\/code> / <code>scripts/magenta_key.py<\/code> (key + despill). For a sheet on a flat non-magenta background, key it first with <code>mgp cutout in.png -o out.png<\/code> (rembg), then <code>mgp image slice out.png -o kit/<\/code>. rembg + the U2-Net model are installed once with <code>mgp install cutout<\/code> (own venv <code>&lt;home&gt;/venv-cutout<\/code>, packages locked in <code>render/py/requirements-cutout.txt<\/code>, model <code>u2net<\/code> in <code>render/py/model-manifest.json<\/code> with sha256, CPU, ~0.6 GB); a venv with rembg made by hand (e.g. <code>~/vrb<\/code>) is registered with <code>mgp install cutout --adopt<\/code>. <code>mgp cutout<\/code> then finds it through <code>install.json<\/code> (no <code>--python<\/code> needed). The magenta key and <code>slice<\/code> despill the key colour along the edge by default (<code>--no-despill<\/code>; <code>references/image.md<\/code> 3.6 item 6). Reference algorithm (proven):<\/p>\n<ol><li>Background = median of the pixels 3 px in from all four edges; d = RGB distance to the background; d &gt; tol (28) = piece.<\/li><li>Open 3 x 3, close 5 x 5, flood fill from the corners; closed holes inside a piece are filled.<\/li><li>Components with an area of at least minarea are kept (600 px at 1K, 2400 px at 2K).<\/li><li>Soft alpha = clip((d - 0.6 tol) / (0.8 tol), 0, 1) + Gaussian 3 x 3 sigma 0.7.<\/li><li>Order by row band (y // 60), then x; names from <code>--names<\/code> (a warning when the count does not match); output PNG + <code>meta.json<\/code> (w, h, sheet, x, y).<\/li><\/ol>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>mgp image slice assets/src/k1.png -o assets/pieces/k1 --magenta --min-area 2400 --names &lt;name1&gt;,&lt;name2&gt;   # magenta sheet\nmgp image slice assets/src/k1_cutout.png -o assets/pieces/k1 --min-area 2400 --names &lt;name1&gt;,&lt;name2&gt;     # already has alpha<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">5.2<\/span> Hole filling closes intentional gaps (gaps in a fence, wheel spokes); those gaps are opened again (recipe from the papercut-mv method 5.1): inside the object mask, look for grey pixels &gt;= 238 with max - min &lt;= 14; flat components &gt;= 40 px (std &lt; 6, mean &gt;= 244) become transparent holes (dilate 3 x 3), and the holes get only a thin paper edge. Figures that touch are separated with a DP seam (<code>seg2<\/code>).<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">5.3<\/span> <strong>Sequence anchors and scale.<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li>ay = sole line; ax = centre of the feet for standing, centre of the head for walking/running. Stored in <code>anchors.json<\/code> {frame: [ax, ay]}.<\/li><li>One shared scale per sheet: s_sheet = target height / median figure height; per-pose scale is not allowed. Across sheets, s_sheet is set so the character has the same height (proof: walk 0.842, wave 0.759). Soles and body centre are locked to the reference pose (proof baseline y 1165).<\/li><\/ul>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">5.4<\/span> <strong>QC.<\/strong> A numbered contact sheet on a magenta/dark grey background + full-res edge crops: no white fringe, grey boxes, specks, or leftover background between the legs.<\/p>"},{"n":6,"title":"Motion","html":"<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">6.1<\/span> <strong>Clock.<\/strong> Video at 24 fps; pieces and characters on twos: tq = floor(t x 12) / 12. Boil is seeded from floor(t x 12) and the layer index (deterministic). Text is smooth at 24 fps or follows on twos, as confirmed for the project.<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">6.2<\/span> <strong>Element entrances<\/strong> (proven formula):<\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>p(t)     = target + off x exp(-(tq - t0) / 0.17 s)\nalpha(t) = min(1, (tq - t0) / 0.2 s)\npop      : scale = 1 - 0.08 x exp(-(tq - t0) / 0.17 s)<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The remaining 3% of the distance is reached at about 0.58 s, so settle = 14-16 frames. Assembly recipe for a 5 s landmark scene:<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Group<\/th><th>Origin, off<\/th><th>t0 (s)<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Clouds; hills, ground<\/td><td>Side 300-380 px; bottom 150-320 px<\/td><td>0.15-0.45; 0.50-0.88<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Buildings, rocks; hero<\/td><td>Bottom 120-220 px; pop<\/td><td>1.02-1.68; 1.80<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Foreground; label<\/td><td>Bottom 260-280 px; left 420 px<\/td><td>2.15-2.52; 3.00<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>The sun comes down from the top after the sky. Stagger 0.1-0.27 s, from far to near.<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">6.3<\/span> <strong>Shot grammar.<\/strong> Share of the duration: 0-10% empty plate or transition tail; 8-32% first subject; 22-52% supporting elements in narrative order; 45-70% labels; the rest holds, at least 25% of the shot duration.<\/p>\n<ul><li>Frame 0 of the video = thumbnail + hook: the hero or one bold line is already showing, never empty.<\/li><li>The camera moves at the start (push 2-10%, pull about 30% for a reveal) and is still in the second half. Layer zoom zl = z^(0.25 + 0.75 k) around the hero pivot; camera pan = camX x k.<\/li><li>Something changes every 3-5 s; beats of 2-3 s. Idle motion only for decoration (±5-7 px, period about 5 s). Trees stay still; leaves may move.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">6.4<\/span> <strong>Character sequence motion<\/strong> (sequence sheet method; the split-pin rig was rejected):<\/p>\n<ol><li>One sheet per action; every body part changes in every frame.<\/li><li>Side walk = contact, down, passing, then the same for the other leg (6 frames = 1 cycle = 0.5 s on twos, reads as hurried). A relaxed walk T 1.0-1.1 s (gait of 119 steps/minute) needs 12 frames or each drawing held for 4 frames; ask the user which.<\/li><li>Slide speed = 2 x stride length / T (measured from sprite x scale), so the feet do not skate. Sliding a still drawing is not allowed.<\/li><li>Wave: frames 1-6 once, then ping-pong 5-4-5-6. A change of action gets 1-2 held drawings. Turning around needs its own 3-4 frame sheet.<\/li><li>Running seen from behind: the character rises and gets smaller (evidence 1.83 s: sole y 1345 to 1110, scale 1.28 to 0.98), bob -7 px x scale on drawings 2 and 4.<\/li><li>Blank face: a mouth kit of 8 visemes (X, A, E, O, U, MBP, FV, smile) + eyebrows pasted per frame from the head centre and face width per drawing; 3/4 = sticker shifted and squashed, profile = half a sticker, back view without a sticker. The mouth order comes from per-letter timing (6.8), at least 2 frames (1 drawing on twos) per shape.<\/li><li>Crowds: two states per person (calm, reaction), switching from the centre outwards at 0.07 s intervals + a jump of 14 px x sin(pi u) over 0.25 s, with a contact shadow, never all at once.<\/li><\/ol>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">6.5<\/span> <strong>AE principles<\/strong> (<code>motion-graphics<\/code> chapter 2): timing 2-4 frames sharp, 6-8 fast, 12-15 normal, 20-30 heavy; anticipation and arcs; overlap 30-50%; exits 40-50% shorter than entrances; a title that lands is fully still. Advanced principles v2.4 (<code>motion-graphics/references/advanced-principles.md<\/code>): spring presets <code>bouncy<\/code> / <code>snappy<\/code> / <code>heavy<\/code> (ease <code>\"spring.snappy\"<\/code>), <code>inertia()<\/code> for a damped bounce from the incoming velocity, <code>delay()<\/code> / stagger for follow-through, squash with constant volume (sx·sy = 1, <code>stretch<\/code>), QC curves with <code>mgp curves<\/code>, rigid origami folding (<code>origamiStyle: \"rigid\"<\/code>, the default), <code>MGP.field<\/code> (cloner + effector + field), FACS expressions (<code>MGP.face<\/code>).<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">6.6<\/span> <strong>Character consistency: model sheet + motion library.<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol><li>Every main character or object with a face has one model sheet (T4 f): 4 views (front, 3/4, side, back) at one scale, soles on one baseline, + 3 close-ups (head/face, hands, a costume detail or signature prop). A 5-view turnaround is made only when a scene needs a 3/4 back view.<\/li><li>The model sheet's job ID is attached as image_references in every job that contains that character, together with an identical character description (4.4 item 2). The character's height in the 720 w world and s_sheet for each action are recorded once and used in every episode.<\/li><li>The motion library per character is kept outside the episode folder (e.g. <code>&lt;series folder&gt;\\library\\&lt;character&gt;\\<\/code>): model sheet, sheet per action, cut-out sprites, <code>anchors.json<\/code>, face kit, and <code>library.json<\/code>. Stage 4 reads the library first: actions that already exist are reused at no credit cost; only new actions go on the G3 job list. A new action enters the library after it passes T9a.<\/li><li>The reverse direction is made with a horizontal mirror only when the costume and props are symmetrical; its status is written in the library.<\/li><\/ol>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>{\n  &quot;character&quot;: &quot;hero&quot;, &quot;world_height_720&quot;: &quot;&lt;measured&gt;&quot;,\n  &quot;model_sheet&quot;: {&quot;job&quot;: &quot;&lt;job id&gt;&quot;, &quot;file&quot;: &quot;model/hero_model.png&quot;},\n  &quot;face_kit&quot;: {&quot;job&quot;: &quot;&lt;job id&gt;&quot;, &quot;dir&quot;: &quot;face/&quot;},\n  &quot;actions&quot;: {\n    &quot;side_walk&quot;: {&quot;job&quot;: &quot;&lt;job id&gt;&quot;, &quot;dir&quot;: &quot;seq/walk/&quot;, &quot;frames&quot;: [&quot;wk1&quot;, &quot;wk2&quot;, &quot;wk3&quot;, &quot;wk4&quot;, &quot;wk5&quot;, &quot;wk6&quot;],\n      &quot;s_sheet&quot;: 0.842, &quot;cycle_s&quot;: 0.5, &quot;stride_px&quot;: &quot;&lt;measured&gt;&quot;, &quot;facing&quot;: &quot;frame right&quot;, &quot;mirror&quot;: true, &quot;passed_T9a&quot;: &quot;&lt;date&gt;&quot;},\n    &quot;wave&quot;: {&quot;job&quot;: &quot;&lt;job id&gt;&quot;, &quot;dir&quot;: &quot;seq/wave/&quot;, &quot;s_sheet&quot;: 0.759, &quot;order&quot;: &quot;1-6, then 5-4-5-6&quot;, &quot;mirror&quot;: false}\n  }\n}<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">6.7<\/span> <strong>Object personification<\/strong> (personification principles from public research, credited in NOTICE, + <code>motion-graphics<\/code> chapter 2; facial expressions can come from FACS AUs in <code>MGP.face<\/code>, <code>motion-graphics/references/advanced-principles.md<\/code> chapter 8).<\/p>\n<ol><li>The object is generated plain without a face (kit or T13 motion set); face = sticker from the object's face kit (T4 d, row 3 eyes), pasted per frame from the object's face anchor as in 6.4 item 6. <strong>Free route without a face kit:<\/strong> the face may be drawn in code on top of the object: eyes = simple shapes (ellipse/blink line) and the mouth from <code>vo.letters.json<\/code> through <code>MGP.lipsync.visemes<\/code> + <code>at(t)<\/code> + <code>lib.drawMouth<\/code> (<code>references/lipsync.md<\/code>), drawn on the canvas at the object's face anchor, in an ink/cream colour that suits the paper. This is a valid replacement for the face kit (tested on a chat bubble with a face in the 30 s ad); still follow item 3 (change mood through a take) and item 5 (eyes lead).<\/li><li>Object rig = parameters over time: position, squash, angle, eye state, mouth (6.8). Mood is written as keyed states, e.g. <code>[[0, \"normal\"], [2.1, \"sad\"]]<\/code>.<\/li><li>A change of mood always goes through one \"take\": anticipation (squash down 2-4 frames), the new face at the deepest squash, then settle with overshoot (<code>motion-graphics<\/code> chapter 2: amp 15, freq 3, decay 5). The face never switches directly.<\/li><li>Squash keeps the area: sx = 1 / sy (constant volume, <code>MGP.core.stretch<\/code>; the old formula sx = 1 + (1 - sy) x 0.5 is only an estimate with an error of ±20%), anchored at the base of the object. Cheap motion without walking: jump (<code>motion-graphics<\/code> 4.1 (<code>motion-graphics/references/walk.md<\/code>): anticipation, push-off, air, landing with damping), lean, spin, wobble.<\/li><li>Never all at once: the eyes lead, the body follows 2-5 frames later; the object's two arms/legs and a crowd of objects do not move together (0.07 s interval as in 6.4 item 7). Blink = the eyes squashed into a line.<\/li><li>Contact: the object stands with a contact shadow (3.2); when it jumps the shadow gets smaller; an object being held sticks to the hand.<\/li><li>One action, one read: the viewer's eye is led to where the action is, the action is fast, the reaction is held; text does not repeat what is already visible.<\/li><\/ol>\n<h4>6.8 Lip-sync from per-letter timing<\/h4>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">6.8<\/span> <strong>Lip-sync from per-letter timing<\/strong> (replaces the Rhubarb recognizer, which is English-only).<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Order<\/th><th>Timing source<\/th><th>Contents<\/th><th>Notes<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>1<\/td><td>ElevenLabs <code>POST /v1/text-to-speech/{voice_id}/with-timestamps<\/code><\/td><td><code>alignment.characters<\/code>, <code>character_start_times_seconds<\/code>, <code>character_end_times_seconds<\/code><\/td><td>Support in the project's VO model is checked at G2<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>2<\/td><td>ElevenLabs <code>POST /v1/forced-alignment<\/code> (audio file + script)<\/td><td><code>characters[]<\/code> and <code>words[]<\/code> with text, start, end<\/td><td>For a final VO that is already done<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>3<\/td><td><code>mgp transcribe vo.wav --language id<\/code> (local), then <code>mgp align vo.wav --script script.txt<\/code> for per-word and per-letter timing; any other transcriber that gives per-word timing also works<\/td><td>Per-word timing; letters are spread evenly inside each word<\/td><td>Coarsest; weak spots are marked CHECK BY EAR<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>4<\/td><td>Rhubarb Lip Sync 1.14.0 <code>-r phonetic<\/code><\/td><td>Visemes A-H, X<\/td><td>Fallback if the user chooses it: A to MBP, B/C/H to E, D to A, E to O, F to U, G to FV, X to X<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>API calls use the key in the user's computer environment (PowerShell); it is never written in a project file.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Timing of one speaker's line (warning).<\/strong> A character that speaks only one line of the VO takes its letters and words from the aligned timing of the <strong>final<\/strong> audio (<code>vo.letters.json<\/code> / <code>vo.words.json<\/code> of the <code>vo.wav<\/code> that is mixed in), cut by the first word's <code>start<\/code> and the last word's <code>end<\/code>. Do not cut the audio or the timing by <code>vo.parts/parts.json<\/code> (<code>start<\/code> is only an estimate, and <code>vo.parts/NN.wav<\/code> are before the <code>--speed<\/code> tempo change), and never cut after an <code>atempo<\/code>: with ffmpeg <code>-ss/-to<\/code> go before <code>-i<\/code>, <code>atempo<\/code> after the cut (P3 ad: the cup's line held the next line's audio and the mouth moved on words it did not say). When a line is cut out as its own file, align that file again (<code>mgp align line.wav --script line.txt<\/code>) and build the visemes from that. Details: <code>references/audio.md<\/code> 10.2a.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Letter (Indonesian)<\/th><th>Kit mouth (T4 d)<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>a<\/td><td>A<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>i, e, é, y<\/td><td>E<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>o<\/td><td>O<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>u, w<\/td><td>U<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>m, b, p<\/td><td>MBP<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>f, v<\/td><td>FV<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Other consonants (including ng, ny, kh, sy)<\/td><td>E, weight 0.5<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Pause of at least 2 drawings (0.17 s), punctuation, emotion tag <code>[...]<\/code><\/td><td>X<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>End of a happy beat (T3)<\/td><td>Smile<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Each on-twos drawing uses the shape that fills the most of its window (consonants are weighted 0.5, so vowels decide more); m, b, p must show closed for at least 1 drawing; short gaps are closed by the previous shape. The 0.5 weight and the 2-drawing pause are starting values, tuned in the G4 pilot. The output <code>visemes/&lt;block&gt;_&lt;character&gt;.json<\/code> holds [{t, v}] and is pasted with the sticker rule in 6.4 item 6.<\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code># viseme_id.py: per-letter timing to kit mouths per on-twos drawing\n# python -I viseme_id.py vo.letters.json &lt;duration_s&gt; visemes/b1_hero.json\nimport json, sys\n\nVIS = {&quot;a&quot;: &quot;A&quot;, &quot;o&quot;: &quot;O&quot;, &quot;u&quot;: &quot;U&quot;, &quot;w&quot;: &quot;U&quot;, &quot;f&quot;: &quot;FV&quot;, &quot;v&quot;: &quot;FV&quot;,\n       **dict.fromkeys(&quot;ieéy&quot;, &quot;E&quot;), **dict.fromkeys(&quot;mbp&quot;, &quot;MBP&quot;)}\n\ndef visemes(chars, starts, ends, dur, draw=12, gap_min=2 / 12, w_kons=0.5):\n    seg, tag = [], False\n    for c, a, b in zip(chars, starts, ends):\n        if c == &quot;[&quot;: tag = True\n        if tag:                                  # emotion tags ([excited], [sighs]) are not spoken\n            tag = c != &quot;]&quot;; continue\n        if c.lower().isalpha():\n            seg.append([a, b, VIS.get(c.lower(), &quot;e&quot;)])   # lowercase &quot;e&quot; = other consonant, shown as E\n    for i in range(len(seg) - 1):                # short gaps are closed by the previous letter\n        if seg[i + 1][0] - seg[i][1] &lt; gap_min:\n            seg[i][1] = seg[i + 1][0]\n    n = int(round(dur * draw))\n    ov = lambda a, b, k: max(0.0, min(b, (k + 1) / draw) - max(a, k / draw))\n    out = []\n    for k in range(n):                           # the shape that fills the most of the drawing window\n        cov = {}\n        for a, b, v in seg:\n            V, w = (&quot;E&quot;, w_kons) if v == &quot;e&quot; else (v, 1.0)\n            if ov(a, b, k) &gt; 0: cov[V] = cov.get(V, 0) + w * ov(a, b, k)\n        out.append(max(cov, key=cov.get) if cov else &quot;X&quot;)\n    for a, b, v in seg:                          # m, b, p closed for at least 1 drawing\n        ks = [k for k in range(n) if ov(a, b, k) &gt; 0]\n        if v == &quot;MBP&quot; and ks and &quot;MBP&quot; not in [out[k] for k in ks]:\n            out[max(ks, key=lambda k: ov(a, b, k))] = &quot;MBP&quot;\n    return [{&quot;t&quot;: round(k / draw, 4), &quot;v&quot;: v} for k, v in enumerate(out)]\n\ndef load(p):                                     # with-timestamps, forced-alignment, or words only\n    d = json.load(open(p))\n    if isinstance(d, dict) and &quot;alignment&quot; in d: d = d[&quot;alignment&quot;]\n    if isinstance(d, dict) and &quot;character_start_times_seconds&quot; in d:\n        return d[&quot;characters&quot;], d[&quot;character_start_times_seconds&quot;], d[&quot;character_end_times_seconds&quot;]\n    if isinstance(d, dict) and d.get(&quot;characters&quot;):\n        C = d[&quot;characters&quot;]; return [c[&quot;text&quot;] for c in C], [c[&quot;start&quot;] for c in C], [c[&quot;end&quot;] for c in C]\n    if isinstance(d, list) and d and (&quot;phone&quot; in d[0] or &quot;letter&quot; in d[0]):   # mgp voice/align: vo.letters.json\n        k = &quot;phone&quot; if &quot;phone&quot; in d[0] else &quot;letter&quot;\n        return [x[k] for x in d], [x[&quot;start&quot;] for x in d], [x[&quot;end&quot;] for x in d]\n    ch, st, en = [], [], []\n    for w in (d[&quot;words&quot;] if isinstance(d, dict) else d):   # letters spread evenly inside the word\n        s = w[&quot;text&quot;].strip(); n = max(1, len(s))\n        for i, c in enumerate(s):\n            ch.append(c); st.append(w[&quot;start&quot;] + (w[&quot;end&quot;] - w[&quot;start&quot;]) * i / n)\n            en.append(w[&quot;start&quot;] + (w[&quot;end&quot;] - w[&quot;start&quot;]) * (i + 1) / n)\n    return ch, st, en\n\nif __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:\n    json.dump(visemes(*load(sys.argv[1]), float(sys.argv[2])), open(sys.argv[3], &quot;w&quot;))<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Synthetic test 02-11-2026 (each letter 0.07 s): a short two-word Indonesian phrase with a tag and a comma gives X during the tag, MBP on the closed-lip letters (b, m), open mouth shapes (A, U, E) on the vowels and other consonants, and X at the comma pause. A test with a real VO is done in the G4 pilot.<\/p>\n<h4>6.9 Blocking stage (G4 pilot, v2.4)<\/h4>\n<p>The pilot is reviewed in two steps, like blocking then spline in 2D/3D animation.<\/p>\n<ol><li><strong>Blocking (pose to pose, stepped).<\/strong> Write motion as key poses: every action ENDS exactly at its key time. Key times per scene are written in <code>project.json<\/code> <code>scenes[].poses<\/code> (seconds, relative to the scene start) and in the composition (<code>POSES<\/code> in the <code>index.html<\/code> made by <code>mgp new<\/code>). Blocking mode holds the last key pose (no interpolation): composition variable <code>blocking<\/code>.<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>mgp contact index.html --var blocking=true --times poses -o review/blocking.png   # one cell per key pose\nmgp contact index.html --var blocking=true --times 0,0.75,1.5 -o review/b_s02.png  # given seconds\nmgp render index.html --var blocking=true --size 540x960 --crf 26 --preset veryfast -o review/blocking.mp4<\/code><\/pre><\/div> What is judged: the silhouette of each pose, staging (one focus), screen direction, rough timing (spacing between keys), and whether the story reads without interpolation. The user approves (G4 step a) before spline. Sequence sheet characters: key pose = the contact/passing drawing or the extreme drawing of each action.<\/li><li><strong>Spline.<\/strong> Ease, overlap, follow-through, springs (<code>spring.bouncy|snappy|heavy<\/code>), inertia, and delay are added without moving the key poses. QC: <code>mgp curves index.html -o review/curves.png<\/code> (no accidental linear motion, pops only at impacts/cuts, constant squash volume), then a contact sheet + a clip with audio. The user approves (G4 step b) and block 1 becomes the reference.<\/li><li>Block 2 onwards uses the same order, but a REPORT is enough (the blocking sheet + curves go into the T9a report).<\/li><li><strong>Narrated video: key poses from VO timing.<\/strong> Ads/explainers are driven by <code>vo.words.json<\/code>, so keys are not invented: (a) mark the keywords per sentence (nouns/verbs that must be seen); (b) key pose = the <code>start<\/code> of that word minus the anticipation (the action lands AS the word is heard, 6.2) or the word's <code>end<\/code> for a reaction; (c) one key per 0.8-1.5 s, scenes without keywords use the sentence pauses; (d) write the keys in <code>project.json<\/code> <code>scenes[].poses<\/code> and the composition's <code>POSES<\/code> BEFORE writing the spline. The order stays: blocking from the VO keys → approved → spline (rule 3.10).<\/li><li><strong>Keys on the frame grid.<\/strong> Every key time is rounded to the 1/fps grid (24 fps: multiples of 0.041667 s; on twos: 1/12 s): <code>k = Math.round(t * fps) / fps<\/code>. A key between two frames makes <code>mgp contact --times poses<\/code> capture the frame before, so the blocking sheet shows the PREVIOUS pose (30 s ad test: empty scenes at 13.08 and 25.29 s). VO word times are also rounded before they become keys.<\/li><\/ol>"},{"n":7,"title":"Kinetic typography","html":"<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">7.1<\/span> Text is only made in code, never in an AI image.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Role<\/th><th>Font, size (720 w)<\/th><th>Motion<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Kicker<\/td><td>Poppins SemiBold caps, tracking 0.3 em, 14-18 px<\/td><td>Fade 4 frames<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Headline<\/td><td>Poppins Bold 46-64 px<\/td><td>Fade-rise per word 4-6 frames, rising 6-8 px, at the VO word timestamp<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Keyword<\/td><td>Playfair Display Italic / Cormorant Garamond Italic, 1.12 x headline<\/td><td>Highlight strip wipe 6 frames OR draw-on underline 8-16 frames after the word; pulse 5.5% exactly at the word timestamp<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>VO subtitle<\/td><td>Poppins on a cream strip, 3-5 words, 52 px (min 28 px)<\/td><td>Per phrase<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Frame<\/th><th>Spec<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Cream card<\/td><td>#FBF6EA, soft shadow (dy 6 px, blur 12 px, 30%), rotation ±1-1.5°<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Navy card<\/td><td>#1C2B45, torn cream rim #F1E6D0, white text, accent #F3C766<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Strip label<\/td><td>Cream + colour ply (#B23A32 or #E3B04B) offset -4 px, +5 px<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Highlight strip<\/td><td>#F1C75B, torn edge (turbulence + displacement scale 9, or polygon clip-path), from 34% of the word height, height 58%<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Text black #141414, white on dark blocks; the accent only for keywords. Max 2-3 lines, 1-4 words per callout, 3-7 words per kinetic scene.<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">7.2<\/span> <strong>Timing and layout.<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul><li>Words appear at the VO timestamp from a per-word transcript that has been checked against the script; weak spots are marked CHECK BY EAR.<\/li><li>Reading hold = 0.5 s + 0.3 s per word; the last line of the film at least 2 s; exit 0.13-0.25 s.<\/li><li>9:16 safe zone: top 9% empty, bottom at least 20% free of text; for Reels/TikTok every word inside the band 168-1330 px (of 1920), not under the right-hand buttons, subtitles at the bottom of the band.<\/li><li>Minimum text size 2.6% of the short side (19 px at 720 w; <code>mgp check<\/code> checks it automatically: finding \"text below the reading limit\"); a 14-18 px kicker is kept with a written reason or raised to 19 px.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">7.3<\/span> <strong>Fonts.<\/strong> mgp ships 24 OFL fonts, including Poppins (with italic), Playfair Display, Cormorant Garamond, DM Serif Display, and Noto Naskh Arabic (list in <code>render/README.md<\/code>, \"Fonts without internet\"). <code>mgp new --font \"Poppins,Playfair Display\"<\/code> copies the chosen families to <code>assets/fonts/<\/code> + <code>fonts.css<\/code>, so the project runs from file:// and without internet. Fonts outside the bundle (Amiri, Aref Ruqaa, Reem Kufi Ink, Lateef; npm package <code>@fontsource<\/code>) are copied to <code>assets/fonts/<\/code> and loaded with <code>@font-face<\/code>. <code>mgp check<\/code> flags text that relies only on a system font or the internet.<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">7.4<\/span> <strong>Arabic.<\/strong> Amiri 700. Name calligraphy: ﷴ (U+FDF4) large + ﷺ (U+FDFA) at 0.26 x ﷴ at the top left of the medallion, centred with getBBox after the font is ready. Text and harakat are checked letter by letter against the source in a crop.<\/p>"},{"n":8,"title":"Transitions and set changes","html":"<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Intent<\/th><th>Transition<\/th><th>Duration<\/th><th>How<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Change of location<\/td><td>Torn-paper wipe<\/td><td>0.5 s<\/td><td>Seeded torn clip-path + torn strip from the kit<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Time passes<\/td><td>Page-turn<\/td><td>0.7 s<\/td><td>Wipe + fold shadow of width sin(pi u)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Change of chapter<\/td><td>Shutters<\/td><td>0.65 s<\/td><td>Slats close, swap at 50%, open<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Focus<\/td><td>Iris<\/td><td>0.55 s<\/td><td>circle() from the hero centre, 0 to 72%<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Continuity<\/td><td>Slide<\/td><td>0.45 s<\/td><td>Sheet enters with an edge shadow<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Object moves to a new set<\/td><td>Bridge carry-over<\/td><td>0.5-0.9 s<\/td><td>Object stays on an overlay, the old set tears/fades behind it<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Break in meaning<\/td><td>Torn split<\/td><td>22 frames<\/td><td>Two halves rotate ±10°, shift ±20% of the width<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Impact<\/td><td>Cut<\/td><td>0<\/td><td>On the fastest frame<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<ol><li>Progress uses smoothstep. A crossfade of the whole scene is not allowed; a dissolve is only for a change of time in the same set.<\/li><li>The tail of the outgoing shot and the pause before the VO comes in are each at least as long as the transition.<\/li><li>Every change of location uses a real new set. One film uses 3-5 kinds of motivated transition; at most a third of the joins are cuts.<\/li><li>The next scene's host starts earlier by the transition duration, with a higher z-index; the reveal uses clip-path on an inner wrapper. Ready-made transitions: <code>MGP.causalTransitions<\/code> and <code>MGP.paperFoldTransition<\/code> (11.5); ready-made transition blocks are listed with <code>mgp catalog --category transitions<\/code> and inserted with <code>mgp add &lt;name&gt;<\/code> (folder <code>blocks/<\/code>).<\/li><li><strong>Transitions that clone the scene<\/strong> (<code>transitions/tear<\/code>, <code>paper-fold<\/code>, <code>shatter<\/code>, <code>beat-freeze<\/code>, <code>morph-swap<\/code>, <code>parallax-zoom<\/code>, <code>before-after<\/code>; accents <code>accents/echo<\/code>, <code>accents/long-arrival<\/code>) make a DOM copy of the source scene. That copy: loses every <code>id<\/code> (CSS based on <code>#id<\/code> no longer applies), carries <code>class=\"clip\"<\/code> + <code>data-start<\/code> so the runtime can hide it outside its original range, and has empty canvases (the state at load, not at the time of the transition). So: (a) <code>from<\/code> = an inner non-<code>.clip<\/code> container (e.g. <code>#s05-content<\/code>), not the scene host; (b) styles of the source scene are written with <strong>classes<\/strong>, not <code>#id<\/code>; (c) canvas content and numbers drawn per frame (e.g. <code>data/counter<\/code>) are not copied, so pick a non-cloning transition (<code>paper-collage<\/code>, <code>shutter<\/code>, clip-path torn wipe) for scenes with a canvas; (d) check the middle frame of the transition with <code>mgp frame --t &lt;middle&gt; --scale 2<\/code> (30 s ad test).<\/li><\/ol>"},{"n":9,"title":"Maps and routes","html":"<ol><li>A map is never drawn by AI. Coastlines come from the bundled Natural Earth data (<code>MGP.map<\/code>, data in <code>motion-graphics/lib/map-data/<\/code>; no npm), projected with a projection fitted to the frame; the aspect ratio is kept and the viewBox is never stretched.<\/li><li>City coordinates (lat, lng) come from a verified source and are written in the scene data together with that source. Land = textured kit paper + shadow; sea = flat plane; labels from code.<\/li><li>The route grows from its tip: <code>pathLength=\"100\"<\/code>, <code>stroke-dasharray=\"100 120\"<\/code>, dashoffset 110 to 0. The length is computed once at init (<code>getTotalLength()<\/code> can be 0 before layout).<\/li><li>The traveller sits at the head of the line and faces the smoothed tangent direction, turning at most 120 degrees per second. The table of positions and angles per frame is computed once at init.<\/li><li>At each city the route stops, the label appears on the VO word, then holds. The camera follows the route head within limits, so the map edge is never visible.<\/li><li>Default map = <code>MGP.map<\/code> (Natural Earth) + <code>MGP.routeMap<\/code> (growing route, vehicle facing the tangent). Satellite imagery is offered only as an option when the user supplies the source and its licence; this package does not ship a basemap baking script.<\/li><\/ol>"},{"n":10,"title":"Audio","html":"<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item<\/th><th>Rule<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>VO<\/td><td>Normalised; per-word transcript = the clock for text and action; 0.8 s pause between blocks<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>SFX<\/td><td>Only on visible actions (paper slide, pop, tear, step); peak 1 frame after the picture; 2-3 alternating takes; 5 dB down while the VO speaks<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Music<\/td><td>Per project (a narration-only project may have no music); when used, about 0.14 volume under the VO<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Master<\/td><td>-14 LUFS, true peak -1.5 dBTP; the report says the audio was measured, not listened to<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>VO + SFX mix: a copy-ready recipe in 10.4 (ffmpeg <code>sidechaincompress<\/code> for ducking, then <code>mgp audio normalize<\/code>), checked with <code>mgp audio measure<\/code> / ffmpeg <code>loudnorm<\/code> <code>print_format=summary<\/code> (T9). <code>mgp audio<\/code> for normalisation and level reports; <code>mgp sfx<\/code> for procedural sound effects, <code>mgp sfx mix<\/code> for an SFX track from a cue list.<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">10.1<\/span> <strong>Narration voice source (decision point, part of G2).<\/strong> The voice is the clock of the video: the timing of text, action and mouth is taken from the VO. Before the stage 3 VO is made, Claude asks for the voice source with four options (chat + interactive question), then waits for the answer:<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option<\/th><th>Contents<\/th><th>Word/syllable/letter timing<\/th><th>Cost<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>a. ElevenLabs<\/td><td>A stock voice, or the user's own cloned voice in their ElevenLabs account. Through the ElevenLabs connector (Claude picks the voice with <code>creative_list_voices<\/code>) or the API: <code>mgp voice elevenlabs script.txt --voice &lt;id&gt; -o vo.wav<\/code> (env <code>ELEVENLABS_API_KEY<\/code>, endpoint <code>/with-timestamps<\/code>)<\/td><td>from the ElevenLabs character alignment → <code>vo.words.json<\/code> + <code>vo.letters.json<\/code>; connector output: <code>mgp align<\/code><\/td><td>ElevenLabs credits<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>b. Local voice clone<\/td><td>The user records ~30 s reading <code>voice/reading-template.md<\/code> (<code>mgp voice template<\/code>). Claude inspects it (<code>mgp voice inspect<\/code>), creates a profile (<code>mgp voice clone recording.wav --name &lt;profile&gt;<\/code>), then makes the VO from the script (<code>mgp voice speak script.txt --profile &lt;profile&gt; -o vo.wav --emotion warm<\/code>)<\/td><td><code>mgp align<\/code> automatically → <code>vo.words.json<\/code> + <code>vo.letters.json<\/code><\/td><td>free, local; NVIDIA GPU when present (±4x faster), otherwise CPU (<code>voice/README.md<\/code>)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>c. User recording<\/td><td>The user records the whole narration themselves<\/td><td><code>mgp align<\/code> or <code>mgp transcribe --fix-with script.txt<\/code><\/td><td>free<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>d. No narration<\/td><td>Text and music/SFX only<\/td><td>clock = beat sheet T3<\/td><td>-<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Local clone workflow (option b):<\/p>\n<ol><li><code>mgp voice template [--language en]<\/code> → send the reading text + how to record (quiet room, 20-30 cm, no music, WAV/MP3/M4A 24-48 kHz).<\/li><li><code>mgp voice inspect recording.wav<\/code> → duration ≥ 20 s, SNR, clipping, loudness, sample rate, and the consent sentence. ERROR = ask for a new recording, using the message from that command.<\/li><li><code>mgp voice clone recording.wav --name &lt;profile&gt;<\/code> → <code>&lt;profile folder&gt;/&lt;profile&gt;/<\/code> (clean reference, model conditionals, <code>profile.json<\/code> with the consent time and source hash). The default profile folder is <code>~/.mgp/voice-profiles/<\/code>, outside the skill/plugin folder (an update replaces that folder); an older in-skill <code>voice/profiles/<\/code> that already holds profiles is still used. Move it with env <code>MGP_VOICE_PROFILES<\/code> (or <code>--folder<\/code>).<\/li><li><code>mgp voice speak script.txt --profile &lt;profile&gt; -o vo.wav [--emotion neutral|warm|energetic|calm] [--speed 1.0]<\/code> → VO at -16 LUFS + word, syllable and letter timing + <code>vo.parts/<\/code> (one WAV per sentence). Brands/abbreviations are spelled through pronunciations (10.3), not by changing the script. Check sentence numbers and spoken spellings first, without a GPU: <code>--dry-run<\/code>.<\/li><\/ol>\n<p>The clone engine is installed once per computer (<code>mgp install voice<\/code>: venv + version-locked models, not in the ZIP; <code>mgp doctor<\/code> gives GPU/CPU advice, <code>references/hardware.md<\/code>). Owner consent given directly outside the recording (a recording without the consent sentence, where the voice owner asks for it themselves): <code>mgp voice clone recording.wav --name &lt;profile&gt; --manual-consent --statement \"&lt;who owns the voice, when, how consent was given&gt;\"<\/code>; the statement is recorded in <code>profile.json<\/code>. The user's original recording is not copied into the project or pack folder; it is read from where it is.<\/p>\n<ol><li><code>mgp voice score vo.wav --profile &lt;profile&gt; --script script.txt<\/code> → raw and <strong>normalised<\/strong> WER/CER (the G2 numbers), speaker similarity, and a <strong>CHECK BY EAR<\/strong> list (words that still differ after normalisation + seconds + sentence number). The G2 report uses the normalised numbers, mentions the raw numbers, and lists the CHECK BY EAR items that have been listened to. A real mispronunciation: redo only that sentence (10.2); a correct sound that the ASR spells differently: add a variant in <code>pronunciations.txt<\/code> (10.3).<\/li><\/ol>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">10.2<\/span> <strong>Redo one sentence.<\/strong> <code>mgp voice speak script.txt -o vo.wav --sentence 3 [--seed N | --tries 4]<\/code> remakes sentence 3 only (also <code>2,5<\/code> or <code>2-4<\/code>), reuses the other sentences from <code>vo.parts/<\/code>, joins them with the same pauses, then rewrites the loudness, <code>vo.words.json<\/code> and <code>vo.letters.json<\/code>. <code>--tries N<\/code> makes N candidates in one model load and picks the one with the best score (normalised WER from ASR + plausible duration + signs of token repetition); the other candidates are kept in <code>vo.parts/candidates/<\/code> for listening. With <code>--sentence<\/code> the current take is scored as one more candidate and is only replaced when a new one scores strictly better (the scores of the current take and the best new one are printed; <code>--force<\/code> takes the best new one anyway). Afterwards run <code>mgp voice score<\/code> again. Details: <code>voice/README.md<\/code>, section Speak.<\/p>\n<p>10.2a <strong>Cutting one line out of the VO (warning).<\/strong> Cut on the timeline of the audio you cut, never on a re-timed one. <code>vo.wav<\/code> is already tempo-changed by <code>--speed<\/code> (atempo on the joined parts), and <code>vo.words.json<\/code> holds the times of that final file; <code>vo.parts/NN.wav<\/code> are the sentences *before* the tempo change (natural speed). So: (a) a line out of <code>vo.wav<\/code> = the first word's <code>start<\/code> to the last word's <code>end<\/code> from <code>vo.words.json<\/code> (aligned on the final audio), not <code>parts.json<\/code> <code>start<\/code> + the part duration; (b) a line from <code>vo.parts/NN.wav<\/code> that must play at the VO speed gets its own <code>atempo<\/code> <strong>after<\/strong> the cut. With ffmpeg, put <code>-ss/-to<\/code> <strong>before<\/strong> <code>-i<\/code> (input seeking: times on the source) and the <code>atempo<\/code> in <code>-af<\/code>; <code>-ss/-to<\/code> after <code>-i<\/code> together with <code>-af atempo<\/code> measures the cut on the output timeline and takes in audio of the next line (P3 ad: the cup's line 11 carried line 12, \"Simulasi tiga dimensi\"). Check every cut by ear or with <code>mgp transcribe<\/code> before the lip-sync is built on it. <code>mgp<\/code> itself cuts in that order (the render mixer trims on the source, then re-times).<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">10.3<\/span> <strong>Pronunciations (TTS helper spellings).<\/strong> The script is written normally (\"Claude\", \"brief-nya\", \"260\"). Spoken spellings live in the project's <code>pronunciations.txt<\/code> (<code>Claude = Klod<\/code>, <code>Motion Graphics = Mosyen Grafiks<\/code>; right of <code>|<\/code> = ASR variants that count as correct), in <code>--pronounce \"a=b; c=d\"<\/code>, or in <code>voice/pronunciations-default.txt<\/code> (read automatically). Numbers are spelled out automatically. The text in <code>vo.words.json<\/code> stays the original script, so captions never show the helper spellings. The Claude case and how to handle \"clock/cloud\" from the ASR: <code>voice/README.md<\/code>.<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">10.4<\/span> <strong>VO + SFX mix with ducking (copy-ready recipe).<\/strong> SFX are ducked automatically while the VO speaks (sidechain), then mastered to -14 LUFS / -1.5 dBTP. Replace <code>adelay<\/code> with the VO start in the video (ms), and <code>-t<\/code> with the video duration:<\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>mgp sfx mix assets/audio/sfx.json -o assets/audio/sfx.wav          # SFX track from cues\nffmpeg -y -i assets/audio/vo.wav -i assets/audio/sfx.wav -filter_complex \\\n  &quot;[0:a]adelay=300:all=1,apad,asplit=2[vo][sc];[1:a]volume=-3dB[sfx];\\\n   [sfx][sc]sidechaincompress=threshold=0.03:ratio=6:attack=15:release=300[sfxd];\\\n   [vo][sfxd]amix=inputs=2:normalize=0,alimiter=limit=0.84[mix]&quot; \\\n  -map &quot;[mix]&quot; -t 30 -ar 48000 -ac 2 assets/audio/mix_rough.wav\nmgp audio normalize assets/audio/mix_rough.wav -o assets/audio/mix.wav --lufs=-14 --tp=-1.5\nmgp audio measure assets/audio/mix.wav                                    # note LUFS, LRA, peak in T9<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<ul><li>Tested on the VO + SFX of a 30 s ad: -15.2 LUFS raw → -13.9 LUFS, peak -1.7 dBFS, LRA 2.5 LU. Write negative option values with <code>=<\/code> (<code>--lufs=-14<\/code>).<\/li><li><code>threshold<\/code> 0.02-0.05 (smaller ducks sooner), <code>ratio<\/code> 4-8, <code>release<\/code> 250-400 ms so the SFX do not \"pump\" between words. Target: SFX ±5 dB under the VO while it speaks, full level in the pauses; SFX more than 10 dB under the VO are inaudible (test F: ±-27 LUFS, too quiet).<\/li><li>Music (when the project uses it) goes in as a third input with the same <code>sidechaincompress<\/code> chain and a starting <code>volume<\/code> of about -18 dB.<\/li><li>Put <code>mix.wav<\/code> in the composition as a single audio track (the separate VO and SFX are no longer added), then render.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">10.5<\/span> <strong>Speech rate for script estimates.<\/strong> Work out the script length from the rate of the voice source in use, then measure again from the first VO:<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Source<\/th><th>Rate<\/th><th>Origin of the number<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>First reference VO v1 (old voice source, not the local clone)<\/td><td>2.06 words/s<\/td><td>SKILL v2.3<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Local Chatterbox clone, a test profile, <code>--speed 1.0<\/code><\/td><td>1.72 words/s (61 words = 35.4 s)<\/td><td>final test of the 30 s ad, 06-11-2026<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>The same local clone, <code>--speed 1.12<\/code><\/td><td>±1.9 words/s (53 words = 28.2 s)<\/td><td>same<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>For a 30 s VO with the local clone: ±50-53 words at <code>--speed 1.1<\/code>, not 60-70 words. Counts are per script token (\"260\" = 1 word).<\/p>\n<p><strong>Voice ethics (required).<\/strong> Only clone the user's own voice, or the voice of someone else who has given explicit, recorded consent (the consent sentence at the start of the recording; without that sentence <code>mgp voice clone<\/code> refuses, unless the voice owner gives consent directly and it is recorded with <code>--manual-consent --statement<\/code>). Never imitate public figures, celebrities, religious scholars, officials, or people who have died, even when asked. Voice profiles are kept locally on the user's computer, are not uploaded, are not sent to other services, and are not included in shared project folders; delete one with <code>mgp voice remove &lt;profile&gt;<\/code> when the user asks. Disclose cloned VO as a synthetic voice when the platform requires it.<\/p>"},{"n":11,"title":"Rendering with mgp","html":"<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">11.1<\/span> <strong>Contract<\/strong> (the HTML composition format that mgp reads; compatibility with old compositions is in 11.7): timed elements have <code>class=\"clip\"<\/code>, <code>data-start<\/code>, <code>data-duration<\/code> and show during [start, start + duration); video length = the root's <code>data-duration<\/code>; every composition registers one paused timeline after its tweens (<code>window.__timelines[\"&lt;data-composition-id&gt;\"] = tl<\/code>); no <code>Date.now()<\/code>, unseeded <code>Math.random()<\/code>, fetch, timers, or <code>repeat: -1<\/code>; <code>display<\/code>/<code>visibility<\/code> are not tweened on a <code>.clip<\/code>; elements transformed by mgp.motion have no CSS <code>transform<\/code>; every <code>&lt;audio&gt;<\/code> has an <code>id<\/code>; every font uses an mgp bundled font (<code>mgp new<\/code> copies the chosen ones to <code>assets/fonts/<\/code> + <code>fonts.css<\/code>) or an <code>@font-face<\/code> pointing to <code>assets/fonts/<\/code>; asset paths are <code>assets/&lt;file&gt;<\/code> without a leading <code>/<\/code>; scenes are written in a <code>&lt;template&gt;<\/code> with root <code>#root<\/code>, and host id = scene root id = timeline key; scenes use <code>fromTo<\/code>, not <code>from<\/code>. Collage grain = a seeded PNG, not a footage treatment.<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">11.2<\/span> <strong>Collage pattern<\/strong> (T7). mgp seeks the timeline per frame, so <code>mgp preview<\/code> = the render result.<\/p>\n<ul><li>Every piece = an <code>&lt;img&gt;<\/code> with a fixed id (editable in <code>mgp studio<\/code>); the T6 data is written inline by a Python build script.<\/li><li>One mgp.motion proxy tween runs <code>draw(t)<\/code> through onUpdate; draw(t) computes the pieces from tq and calls <code>mgp.motion.set<\/code> (the old alias <code>gsap.set<\/code> still works). The T8 text tweens go into the same timeline.<\/li><li>A frame sequence is stacked as separate <code>&lt;img&gt;<\/code> elements and only one has opacity 1; <code>src<\/code> is not swapped while seeking.<\/li><li>A scene that looks still/empty on an <code>mgp contact<\/code> sheet is moved to a listener on the <code>mgp-seek<\/code> event (the old names <code>rekam-seek<\/code> and <code>hf-seek<\/code> are still dispatched) or to <code>window.__seek(t)<\/code>. The root gets <code>data-fps=\"24\"<\/code>; 16:9 (1920 x 1080) is recomposed, never squeezed.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">11.3<\/span> <strong>Requirements and installation<\/strong> (once per computer).<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Needed<\/th><th>For<\/th><th>Notes<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Node.js 22+<\/td><td>mgp, <code>lib/build.js<\/code><\/td><td><code>node --version<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>ffmpeg + ffprobe on PATH<\/td><td>Encoding, audio, QC<\/td><td>Or env <code>MGP_FFMPEG<\/code> / <code>MGP_FFPROBE<\/code>; Windows: <code>winget install Gyan.FFmpeg<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge, or Chromium<\/td><td>Frame capture browser<\/td><td>Found automatically (including a Chromium downloaded by Playwright if one happens to be there); another path through <code>--browser &lt;path&gt;<\/code> or env <code>MGP_BROWSER<\/code>. mgp does not download a browser<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Python 3.10+ with numpy + Pillow<\/td><td>Cutout, magenta key, T6 build, other skill scripts<\/td><td><code>pip install -r scripts/requirements.txt<\/code>; skia-python and OpenCV are not required<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Local voice/image engines (optional)<\/td><td>Voice clone, free local images<\/td><td>Once per computer: <code>mgp install voice<\/code> / <code>mgp install image<\/code> (venv + models, not in the ZIP); <code>mgp doctor<\/code> suggests the route (<code>references/hardware.md<\/code>)<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>cd &lt;this skill folder&gt;/render\nnode bin/mgp.mjs --version           # no npm install: the CDP client, 24 fonts and mgp.motion are bundled<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Below, <code>&lt;skill&gt;<\/code> = this skill's folder, and <code>mgp<\/code> = <code>node &lt;skill&gt;/render/bin/mgp.mjs<\/code> (or run <code>npm link<\/code> once in <code>render/<\/code> so the <code>mgp<\/code> command is available everywhere). Everything runs locally: no account, no cloud. Option details and the contract: <code>render/README.md<\/code>.<\/p>\n<p>A new project is opened with <code>mgp new &lt;folder&gt; [--aspect 9:16|16:9] [--fps 24] [--duration 30] [--title \"...\"]<\/code>: standard folders, <code>brief.md<\/code> (T1), <code>STATUS.md<\/code> (T10), <code>project.json<\/code> (<code>references/project.md<\/code>), and an <code>index.html<\/code> that already loads <code>assets/js/motion.js<\/code>, <code>assets/js/mgp.js<\/code>, and the bundled fonts through relative paths (works from file:// and through mgp).<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">11.4<\/span> <strong>Commands<\/strong> (from the project folder; on Windows run them in PowerShell):<\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>node &lt;skill&gt;/render/bin/mgp.mjs check index.html               # contract, duration, size, 404, fonts, risky patterns, determinism\nnode &lt;skill&gt;/render/bin/mgp.mjs check index.html --json\nnode &lt;skill&gt;/render/bin/mgp.mjs contact index.html --from 0 --to 5.2 --n 8 -o review/contact_s03.png\nnode &lt;skill&gt;/render/bin/mgp.mjs frame index.html --t 2.4 --scale 2 -o review/f_2.4.png    # edge crop from a 2x capture\nnode &lt;skill&gt;/render/bin/mgp.mjs preview index.html --open      # scrubber at http://127.0.0.1:4600\nnode &lt;skill&gt;/render/bin/mgp.mjs render index.html --from 12.5 --to 18 --size 540x960 --crf 26 --preset veryfast --workers 4 -o review/s03_v2.mp4\nnode &lt;skill&gt;/render/bin/mgp.mjs render index.html --workers 4 -o out/&lt;name&gt;_9x16.mp4<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>HyperFrames command (for those moving over)<\/th><th>mgp<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td><code>npx hyperframes lint<\/code> / <code>check --json<\/code><\/td><td><code>mgp check index.html<\/code> / <code>mgp check index.html --json<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>snapshot --composition s03 --range 0,5.2 --frames 8<\/code><\/td><td><code>mgp contact index.html --from &lt;data-start s03&gt; --to &lt;+5.2&gt; --n 8<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>snapshot --at 1.2,2.4 --zoom '&lt;x,y,w,h&gt;'<\/code><\/td><td><code>mgp frame index.html --t 1.2 -o f.png<\/code> (once per time; <code>--scale 2<\/code>, then crop)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>preview<\/code><\/td><td><code>mgp preview index.html --open<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>render -c compositions/s03.html --quality draft<\/code><\/td><td><code>mgp render index.html --from &lt;start s03&gt; --to &lt;end s03&gt; --size 540x960 --crf 26 --preset veryfast --workers 4<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>render --quality delivery<\/code><\/td><td><code>mgp render index.html -o out.mp4<\/code> (H.264 CRF 18; <code>.mov<\/code> = ProRes 422 HQ, <code>--format prores --transparent<\/code> = 4444 alpha)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>transcribe<\/code>, <code>normalize-audio<\/code>, <code>add<\/code>, <code>catalog<\/code>, Studio/desktop app<\/td><td><code>mgp transcribe<\/code> / <code>mgp align<\/code>, <code>mgp audio normalize<\/code> / <code>measure<\/code>, <code>mgp add<\/code> / <code>mgp catalog<\/code>, <code>mgp studio<\/code><\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Already in mgp: text fitting <code>window.__mgp.fitTextFontSize<\/code> / <code>measureText<\/code> (old name <code>window.__hyperframes.*<\/code>), variables <code>data-var-*<\/code> + <code>--var<\/code> / <code>--vars<\/code>, looks/LUTs (<code>mgp media<\/code>), transcription and alignment, blocks (<code>mgp add<\/code>/<code>catalog<\/code>), Studio, and 24 bundled fonts. Not included: <code>pretext<\/code> and cloud render/publish; for those, text size can still be computed by hand (<code>getBBox<\/code> / <code>measureText<\/code> after <code>document.fonts.ready<\/code>).<\/p>\n<p>After a render: the file is not empty, the ffprobe duration = the root's <code>data-duration<\/code> (<code>ffprobe -v error -count_frames -show_entries stream=nb_read_frames,duration -of compact out.mp4<\/code>), the decoded frames are looked at; <code>mgp render --hash<\/code> twice with the same hash = a deterministic render. A full render only after the word \"render\"; before the delivery render, <code>mgp preview<\/code> is opened for the user, and the user is told that the composition can be edited with <code>mgp studio<\/code>. Python stays for cutouts, anchors, grain PNGs, the HTML build from T6, and the audio mix; visemes from per-letter timing (6.8); Rhubarb Lip Sync 1.14.0 is only a fallback.<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">11.5<\/span> <strong>Motion module library<\/strong> (skill <code>motion-graphics<\/code>, folder <code>lib/<\/code>; list and contract in <code>motion-graphics<\/code> chapter 13 (<code>motion-graphics/references/lib.md<\/code>) and <code>lib/README.md<\/code>). Before writing canvas motion from scratch, check the modules that already exist: 3D characters + gait, shadows from poses, a one-take 3D park, a docking ship, vector/pixel/origami character styles, a folding crane, a vector character kit, paper-fold and motivated transitions, chibi objects, lip-sync, route maps, kinetic typography, springs, particles, constellations.<\/p>\n<ol><li>Copy <code>lib/dist/mgp.js<\/code> (every module; <code>mgp new<\/code> already copies it) or <code>lib/dist/mgp-packs.js<\/code> (packs only) to the project's <code>assets/js/<\/code>: mgp only serves files under the composition folder (<code>--root<\/code> changes that).<\/li><li>Full-canvas composition: <code>MGP.core.expose(inst)<\/code> fills <code>window.__duration<\/code>, <code>__seek(t)<\/code>, <code>__timeline<\/code>, <code>__timelines[\"main\"]<\/code>, and <code>__ready<\/code>; mgp uses <code>__seek<\/code> as the first driver. <strong>Driver + blocks pattern:<\/strong> when the page uses <code>window.__seek<\/code> (or <code>__timeline<\/code>), mgp v2.4 still seeks every sub-composition timeline in <code>window.__timelines<\/code> (blocks from <code>mgp add<\/code>, scenes) first, then calls <code>__seek(t)<\/code>, so <code>__seek<\/code> may override them (e.g. blocking mode). The driver must not seek the block timelines again with another time. Other renderers (HyperFrames, older versions of this renderer) only seek that driver: <code>mgp add<\/code> blocks on a page with <code>__seek<\/code> stay still there. For a composition that must be portable, register the main timeline in <code>window.__timelines[\"&lt;root id&gt;\"]<\/code> without <code>__seek<\/code> and draw the canvas from that timeline's <code>onUpdate<\/code>. <code>window.__mgpNoSubSeek = true<\/code> turns off the sub-composition seek. Several modules: one object <code>{ duration, ready, seek(t) }<\/code> that calls each module's <code>seek<\/code>, then <code>expose<\/code>. Write <code>window.__fps = 24<\/code> (or <code>data-fps<\/code>) so the fps does not fall back to the default 30.<\/li><li>In a T7 scene: the module draws into a <code>&lt;canvas&gt;<\/code> inside the scene's <code>#root<\/code> and is called from the scene's <code>draw(t)<\/code> through <code>MGP.&lt;module&gt;.render(ctx, t, options)<\/code> or <code>inst.seek(t)<\/code> with local time, so there is still one timeline per composition.<\/li><li>Quick check of a module or pack: <code>node &lt;skill&gt;/render/bin/mgp.mjs contact &lt;motion-graphics folder&gt;/lib/examples/&lt;module&gt;.html --n 12 -o contact.png<\/code>.<\/li><\/ol>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">11.6<\/span> <strong>Canvas composition without a timeline<\/strong> (test 01-11-2026): a canvas page only needs to fill <code>window.__duration<\/code> and <code>window.__seek(t)<\/code> (smallest example in <code>render/README.md<\/code>), then <code>mgp render<\/code>. JPEG/PNG sequences made by other tools (e.g. <code>render.py<\/code> of the motion-graphics skill) are assembled with ffmpeg:<\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>ffmpeg -y -framerate 24 -i fr/f%04d.jpg -i mix.wav -c:v libx264 -preset veryfast -crf 18 -pix_fmt yuv420p -c:a aac -b:a 192k -shortest -movflags +faststart out.mp4<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>On Windows, render commands are run in PowerShell.<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">11.7<\/span> <strong>Compatibility.<\/strong> Compositions written for the HyperFrames format render without changes; the old names are still accepted next to the neutral names. The full table (<code>data-*<\/code> attributes, <code>window.__timelines<\/code>, <code>window.__mgp<\/code> = <code>window.__hyperframes<\/code>, event <code>mgp-seek<\/code> = <code>rekam-seek<\/code> = <code>hf-seek<\/code>, <code>__mgpLottie<\/code>/<code>__mgpAnime<\/code> = <code>__hfLottie<\/code>/<code>__hfAnime<\/code>, global <code>mgp.motion<\/code> (old name <code>Gerak<\/code>) = alias <code>gsap<\/code>, block field <code>aliases<\/code> = <code>nama_lama<\/code> = <code>hyperframes<\/code>) is in <code>render/README.md<\/code>, section \"Compatibility\". New compositions use the neutral names.<\/p>\n<h4>Command map (mgp)<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Need<\/th><th>Command<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Open a project<\/td><td><code>mgp new &lt;folder&gt; [--aspect 9:16|16:9|1:1|4:5|21:9] [--resolution 720|1080|4k] [--fps 24] [--duration 30] [--title \"..\"] [--lang id|en]<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Machine profile, route advice<\/td><td><code>mgp doctor [--json]<\/code>; install the local engines once per computer: <code>mgp install voice|image|cutout|all [--detach]<\/code> (cutout = rembg for <code>mgp cutout<\/code>), status <code>mgp install all --status<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Check a composition + <code>project.json<\/code><\/td><td><code>mgp check index.html [--json]<\/code> · one report: <code>mgp audit &lt;folder&gt;<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Contact sheet / blocking / one frame<\/td><td><code>mgp contact index.html --n 8<\/code> · <code>--var blocking=true --times poses<\/code> · <code>mgp frame --t 2.4 --scale 2<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Motion curve QC<\/td><td><code>mgp curves index.html [--selector \"#a\"] -o review/curves.png<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Preview / visual edit<\/td><td><code>mgp preview index.html --open<\/code> · <code>mgp studio index.html --open<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Review / delivery render<\/td><td><code>mgp render index.html --from a --to b --size 540x960 --crf 26 --preset veryfast --workers 2<\/code> · <code>mgp render index.html -o out/&lt;name&gt;.mp4<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Ready-made blocks (260)<\/td><td><code>mgp catalog --search &lt;word&gt;<\/code> · <code>mgp add &lt;category/name&gt; --into index.html<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Free images (kit, plate, assets)<\/td><td><code>mgp image \"&lt;prompt&gt;\" --magenta --cutout [--style paper] [--tries 3]<\/code> · <code>mgp image kit \"&lt;character&gt;\" -o kit/ --tries 3<\/code> · <code>mgp image plate \"&lt;place&gt;\"<\/code> · <code>mgp image slice ... --rotate hand:180<\/code> · <code>mgp image providers<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Voice / VO<\/td><td><code>mgp voice template|inspect|clone|speak|score|elevenlabs|remove<\/code> · redo one sentence <code>mgp voice speak ... --sentence 3 --tries 4<\/code> · spelling aid <code>pronunciations.txt<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Per-word timing<\/td><td><code>mgp align vo.wav --script script.txt -o vo.words.json<\/code> · <code>mgp transcribe vo.wav --fix-with script.txt<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Audio, SFX, look<\/td><td><code>mgp audio normalize|measure<\/code> · <code>mgp sfx &lt;preset&gt;<\/code> · <code>mgp sfx mix cues.json<\/code> · <code>mgp media look|brighten|effects|lut<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Background removal<\/td><td><code>mgp cutout in.png -o out.png [--key magenta]<\/code> · <code>python -I scripts/magenta_key.py ...<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Lottie<\/td><td><code>mgp export --format lottie<\/code> · <code>mgp play<\/code> · <code>mgp render|frame|check &lt;anim.json&gt;<\/code><\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Old Indonesian command and flag names (<code>rekam baru --rasio<\/code>, <code>rekam cek<\/code>, ...) still work as aliases; write the new names. On the user's laptop, renders use <code>--workers 2<\/code> unless the user asks otherwise; GPU jobs take turns.<\/p>"},{"n":12,"title":"Review and QC","html":"<ol><li>Every shot is sent as a contact sheet (start, 25/50/75%, end), full-res crops of foot contacts and edges, and a clip with audio; the T9 checklist is run per shot and per block.<\/li><li>Review notes: <code>t=4.2 s | place | problem → change | what stays<\/code>, ranked P0 (breaks reading/the contract), P1 (weakens), P2 (polish). Every P0 is fixed, then only the changed parts are snapshotted again.<\/li><li>One fix must not break another part. Frames before and after are compared; if one frame gets worse, work stops and it is reported.<\/li><li>Weaknesses that are still visible are written down honestly; placeholders are labelled in the frame.<\/li><li>REPORT deliveries use the T9a report format, one report per block.<\/li><\/ol>\n<h4>12.1 Pilot and new blocks (v2.4)<\/h4>\n<ol><li>The first pilot delivery = the blocking sheet (<code>mgp contact index.html --var blocking=true --times poses<\/code>), without ease and without secondary detail. Review Results at this stage change poses and timing, not easing (<code>references/motion.md<\/code> 6.9).<\/li><li>After spline: <code>mgp curves<\/code> for the changed range. A \"linear\" finding on motion that should be eased, a \"pop\" outside an impact/cut, or a \"volume\" finding on a squash = fixed before REPORT. Findings that are intended (a ball impact, a cut) are written in the T9a report. Reference numbers: <code>motion-graphics/references/advanced-principles.md<\/code>.<\/li><li>The T9a report contains the paths of the blocking sheet, the contact sheet, the curve graph, and the clip with audio.<\/li><\/ol>\n<h4>Key quality checks<\/h4>\n<p>The full T9 checklist (20 items) and the REPORT conditions per asset type T9a are in <code>references/template.md<\/code>; how to review is in <code>references/review-qc.md<\/code>. The checks that most often decide pass/fail:<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Check<\/th><th>How<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Contract, duration, local fonts, text above the reading limit, determinism, <code>project.json<\/code><\/td><td><code>mgp check index.html<\/code> (0 ERROR)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Clean pieces: no white fringe, grey boxes, specks or magenta edges<\/td><td><code>mgp frame --t &lt;t&gt; --scale 2<\/code>, then crop the edges on a contrasting background<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Feet planted, same character size, no foot sliding, mouth/brows on the face<\/td><td>crop the feet; 3 consecutive frames<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Blocking: key poses read, staging, rough timing<\/td><td><code>mgp contact index.html --var blocking=true --times poses<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Motion curves: no accidental linear motion; breaks only at impacts/cuts; squash keeps volume<\/td><td><code>mgp curves index.html --from &lt;a&gt; --to &lt;b&gt;<\/code>; benchmarks in <code>motion-graphics/references/advanced-principles.md<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>On twos, stagger, settle 14-16 frames, hold at least 25% of the shot<\/td><td><code>mgp contact --from &lt;a&gt; --to &lt;b&gt; --n 8<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Words at their VO timestamps; hold 0.5 s + 0.3 s/word; safe zone<\/td><td>a frame per word<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Lip-sync: m/b/p closed, vowels match the letters, X in pauses<\/td><td>frames at m/b/p words + clip with sound<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Audio -14 LUFS, -1.5 dBTP, SFX -5 dB under the VO (ducking, <code>references/audio.md<\/code> 10.4); no dead air &gt; 0.8 s<\/td><td><code>mgp audio measure<\/code>, ffmpeg loudnorm/silencedetect<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Cloned VO: normalised WER + every CHECK BY EAR listened to<\/td><td><code>mgp voice score vo.wav --profile &lt;p&gt;<\/code>; mispronounced: <code>mgp voice speak ... --sentence N --tries 4<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Duration, fps, resolution of the render; deterministic render<\/td><td>ffprobe; <code>mgp render --hash<\/code> twice<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>All of the above in one report<\/td><td><code>mgp audit &lt;folder&gt; --platform tiktok<\/code> (writes <code>review/audit.md<\/code>)<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>"},{"n":13,"title":"Handover","html":"<ul><li>Every session updates T10 (<code>STATUS.md<\/code> from <code>mgp new<\/code>). When the chat context is around 75%, the full plan is written to a file before compacting.<\/li><li>Parallel sessions reread the handover and the artifact database before writing, with pinned versions.<\/li><li>Locked blocks change only at the user's request.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>References (read when the step comes)<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>File<\/th><th>Read when<\/th><th>Contents (old chapter)<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td><code>references/brief-concept.md<\/code><\/td><td>Stages 1-2: T1 form, 3 T2a concepts, T2 taste, POV; the user writes a one-sentence brief<\/td><td>2 (brief, concept, taste, POV, archetypes), 17.1 (sample briefs)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>references/style.md<\/code><\/td><td>Choosing/keeping a style (house style <strong>Atelier Kertas<\/strong>, 3.0); assembling a scene; the brief asks for pixel art or origami<\/td><td>3 (collage visual system), 17.3<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>references/image.md<\/code><\/td><td>Stages 4-5: free image route (<code>mgp image<\/code>, template kits, <code>--tries N<\/code>, <code>--rotate<\/code>, a pose per image), Higgsfield (G3, settings, STYLE, prompt rules), magenta background<\/td><td>Part A (D, v2.4) + 4, 17.4<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>references/hardware.md<\/code><\/td><td>Project start (<code>mgp doctor<\/code>) and every voice/image/encoder choice; GPU queue<\/td><td>v2.4<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>references/cutout.md<\/code><\/td><td>Stage 6: kit cutter, gaps, anchors and sequence scale, cutout QC<\/td><td>5<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>references/motion.md<\/code><\/td><td>Stages 7-8: on-twos clock, element entrances, shot grammar, character sequences, model sheet + motion library, personification, <strong>blocking then spline<\/strong><\/td><td>6.1-6.7, 6.9 (new)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>motion-graphics/references/advanced-principles.md<\/code><\/td><td>Spline and motion QC: spring presets <code>bouncy<\/code>/<code>snappy<\/code>/<code>heavy<\/code>, inertia, delay, constant volume, curves, rigid origami (<code>origamiStyle: \"rigid\"<\/code>), fields, FACS, SLERP<\/td><td>v2.4<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>references/lipsync.md<\/code><\/td><td>A character/object mouth speaking from per-letter timing (<code>vo.letters.json<\/code>, ElevenLabs alignment, <code>mgp align<\/code>)<\/td><td>6.8<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>references/typography.md<\/code><\/td><td>Text, headlines, subtitles, cards, bundled fonts, Arabic text<\/td><td>7<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>references/transitions.md<\/code><\/td><td>Transitions between scenes<\/td><td>8<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>references/maps.md<\/code><\/td><td>Maps, routes, coordinates<\/td><td>9<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>references/audio.md<\/code><\/td><td>G2 (4 voice options, clone flow, ethics), VO, redo per sentence, pronunciation, SFX, mix + ducking, master, speaking rate<\/td><td>10<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>voice/README.md<\/code><\/td><td>Installing and running the local voice engine, quality, limits<\/td><td>-<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>references/render.md<\/code><\/td><td>Writing a composition (contract, collage patterns), requirements, commands, the <code>lib/<\/code> library, compatibility<\/td><td>11<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>render/README.md<\/code><\/td><td>Every mgp command option, variables, fonts, encoders, compatibility table<\/td><td>-<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>references/project.md<\/code><\/td><td><code>project.json<\/code> schema<\/td><td>v2.4<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>references/review-qc.md<\/code><\/td><td>Every REPORT delivery, review per block, before G4/G5<\/td><td>12<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>references/mistakes.md<\/code><\/td><td>Before G1 and before sending the G4 pilot<\/td><td>14<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>references/branches.md<\/code><\/td><td>Stage 4 (branches A/B/C, prices, credit estimates) and assembling branch B<\/td><td>15<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>references/template.md<\/code> · <code>references/template.id.md<\/code> (ID)<\/td><td>Filling T1-T15: brief, comparison, concepts, shot list, prompts, job log, scene data, composition skeleton, typography, T9/T9a checklists, T10 handover, keyframes, layer breakdown, motion set, pixel art, origami<\/td><td>16<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>references/motion-packs.md<\/code><\/td><td>Stage 2 (pack suggestions per category), style frame/test clip from a pack id, blocks per slot<\/td><td>17.2<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>references/history.md<\/code><\/td><td>Finding when a rule came in<\/td><td>old opening<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>"},{"n":14,"title":"Mistakes that were rejected before","html":"<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rejected<\/th><th>Replacement<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Vector paper drawn in code (childish)<\/td><td>Real paper kit pieces<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>One full AI plate + overlay (not a collage)<\/td><td>A 21:9 kit assembled piece by piece, or a keyframe split into layers (chapter 15)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Split-pin puppet rig, joints/pins visible<\/td><td>Sequence motion from a sheet<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Props with no purpose or floating<\/td><td>Removed, or attached with context<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Sun badge drawn in code<\/td><td>Sun/disc from the kit + glow in code<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Map drawn by AI or viewBox stretched<\/td><td>Natural Earth + real coordinates (chapter 9)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Mouth sticker in the wrong place<\/td><td>Head anchor per frame + contact per frame<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Prompt \"lo-fi anime background painting\" (flat)<\/td><td>Real-paper STYLE block (4.3)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Placeholder effect sent as if final<\/td><td>PLACEHOLDER label in the frame + delivery note<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Layer-split sheet that redraws (oversized layers, pose changed)<\/td><td>Prompt \"COPY, do not redraw\" + KEEP/ERASE percentages + required matching (15.1)<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>"},{"n":15,"title":"Production branches","html":"<p>Every scene picks one branch in T3 (Assets column). Spending the fewest credits is the top priority. Higgsfield preflight prices 01-11-2026: GPT Image 2.5 1K 1.5 and 2K 2.75 per image; Kling 3.0 10 per 5 s 9:16; Seedance 2.5 35 per 5 s 9:16.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Branch<\/th><th>When<\/th><th>Assets<\/th><th>Credits per location<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>A Kit only (cheapest)<\/td><td>Secondary locations, simple scenes<\/td><td>1 kit 21:9 + motion set<\/td><td>±5-8<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>B Reverse stripe<\/td><td>Main locations that must look rich (market, town)<\/td><td>Keyframe 9:16 2K + 3 layer-split sheets 21:9 2K + motion set<\/td><td>±11 + motion set<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>C AI video<\/td><td>2-3 hero shots per episode<\/td><td>Kling 3.0 from an approved still<\/td><td>10 per 5 s<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Estimate for a 3-minute episode: branches A/B about 150 credits (layers and motion sets are reused); all Kling about 460; all Seedance about 1,360 (regenerations not included). These numbers are estimates from assumed counts of locations and motion sets.<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">15.1<\/span> <strong>Branch B: keyframe → layer split → assemble<\/strong> (proof: <code>pasar_rakit_1k.mp4<\/code>):<\/p>\n<ol><li><strong>Keyframe that is easy to split<\/strong> (T11): plan 8-12 layers with regions as a percentage of the frame; gaps between groups; medium detail (small objects grouped); a front object covers at most a third of an object behind it; the main character is not included (or stands in an open area that is easy to erase); an empty floor path for the character; background people already face the intended direction.<\/li><li><strong>Layer split<\/strong> (T12): a 21:9 sheet with four 9:16 panels, keyframe attached. Required: \"COPY from the keyframe, do not redraw\" (same scale, position, pose, facing); every panel has KEEP + a percentage region and a firm ERASE; FILL only for back layers and only their own surface, people/prop layers \"no fill\"; one panel must be the SHELL (roof/sky + floor + end of the aisle, empty); plain magenta #FF00FF, white gutters. Order: sheet 1 shell, aisle end, left row, right row; sheet 2 people; sheet 3 front props + empty plate (spare and reference). Per-layer 9:16 edits (one job per layer, keyframe as canvas: \"IMAGE EDIT of the attached image, not a new drawing ... KEEP ... ERASE ... flat solid magenta ... FILL ...\") only when the user puts accuracy above credits (about 33-36 credits per location).<\/li><li><strong>Cut:<\/strong> panels are separated at the white columns (channel minimum &gt; 225 over more than 85% of the height); 1K panel = 331 x 576 px, 2K about 660 x 1152 px. Alpha = clip((RGB distance to #FF00FF - 70) / 60, 0, 1), erode 3 x 3, blur 0.8. Despill: within 7 px of the edge, R and B are limited to at most G + 35.<\/li><li><strong>Match to the keyframe (required; the model does not keep scale and position across panels):<\/strong> frame 720 x 1280 = keyframe 752 x 1344 x 0.952, centre crop. When OpenCV is installed (optional, <code>pip install opencv-python-headless<\/code>): SIFT 4000-5000 points, Lowe ratio 0.75, <code>estimateAffinePartial2D<\/code> RANSAC 4-5 px; accepted when there are at least 10 inliers and the scale is plausible. Without OpenCV, go straight to the plain panel mapping below. Repeating patterns (zinc roofs, floors, rows of stalls) often fail SIFT: use the plain panel mapping (scale 1280/576, centre shift), then check the overlap against the empty plate. A figure whose pose changed: a manual box at the keyframe position. A layer holding two objects far apart (bananas + crate) is split per component first. An oversized layer is replaced by a better-fitting source (the stall row from the far background layer, split left, right, centre). The still composite is compared side by side with the keyframe before animation.<\/li><li><strong>Assemble from empty<\/strong> (formula 6.2, on twos, layer shadow blur 10 px down 6 px opacity 0.32, grain 0.08, push 5%):<\/li><\/ol>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>t0 (s)<\/th><th>Layer<\/th><th>Entrance<\/th><th>k<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>0<\/td><td>Cream paper + light at the aisle end<\/td><td>already there<\/td><td>0<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>0.10<\/td><td>Floor<\/td><td>rises from below 380 px<\/td><td>0.30<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>0.40<\/td><td>Aisle end<\/td><td>pops from the vanishing point, scale 0.88 to 1<\/td><td>0.25<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>0.65 / 0.80<\/td><td>Left / right row<\/td><td>slides in from its own side 420 px<\/td><td>0.40<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>1.10<\/td><td>Roof<\/td><td>drops 320 px<\/td><td>0.35<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>1.45<\/td><td>Background people<\/td><td>fade 0.5 s, then move away (scale down 5%)<\/td><td>0.45<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>1.65 / 1.80<\/td><td>Left / right seller<\/td><td>rise 90 px from behind the table, small sway<\/td><td>0.55<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>2.05<\/td><td>Buyer<\/td><td>slides in from the right 260 px<\/td><td>0.68<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>2.35<\/td><td>Hanging objects<\/td><td>drop 430 px + a 10 degree swing that damps out<\/td><td>1.05<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>2.55 / 2.70<\/td><td>Crate / basket<\/td><td>rises 420 px / slides in from the right 440 px<\/td><td>1.1<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>3.0<\/td><td>Character<\/td><td>walk-in sequence from outside the frame<\/td><td>0.85<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Assembly rules: back first, front last, the character very last; left row from the left, right row from the right; hanging objects from above; floor objects from below; 0.15-0.3 s between layers; the complete scene is held for at least 25% of the duration.<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">15.2<\/span> <strong>Motion set (stop motion of objects and characters).<\/strong> Whatever moves by itself is drawn as one set of frames (T13) on a plain background: same size, position and baseline; only the moving part changes (\"identical size and position in every frame; only &lt;part&gt; changes\"). A motion set is not cut from a detailed keyframe, because a very detailed picture is hard to split. Character slide speed = 2 x step length / cycle period (market proof: relaxed walk 1 s per cycle, scale 0.55, 360 px/s).<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">15.3<\/span> <strong>Lessons from the market test 01-11-2026.<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Problem<\/th><th>Cause<\/th><th>Rule<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Layer oversized and shifted<\/td><td>The prompt asked for a new panel; the model redrew<\/td><td>\"COPY, do not redraw\" + required matching<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Far background holds the whole row<\/td><td>Ambiguous layer boundary<\/td><td>KEEP/ERASE with percentage regions<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>People turned around<\/td><td>People layer was redrawn<\/td><td>\"same pose, no fill\"<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Cannot be assembled from empty<\/td><td>No shell<\/td><td>One panel must be the shell<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Blur<\/td><td>1K sheet (331 px panel)<\/td><td>2K keyframe and sheets<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Magenta edges<\/td><td>Colour spill<\/td><td>Despill 15.1 item 3<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>"},{"n":17,"title":"Version 2.1 additions","html":"<p>This section adds four things proven on the guide site and its code demos: sample briefs, the motion pack library, pixel art and origami styles in the production flow, and the magenta-background Higgsfield asset trick. The rules in chapters 0-16 still apply.<\/p>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">17.1<\/span> <strong>How to ask Claude (sample briefs).<\/strong> The user does not need technical terms. The one-sentence pattern:<\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>I want to make a motion graphics [video type] about [topic], [seconds] long, aspect ratio [9:16 or 16:9], in a [style] style. It shows [characters / objects / map / chart], with [narration / music].<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field<\/th><th>Read by Claude in<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>video type<\/td><td>Pack category (17.2) and T1<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>duration<\/td><td>T1, shot grammar 6.3<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>aspect ratio<\/td><td>T1, chapter 11<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>style<\/td><td>Taste 2.3 + T2; pixel art T14, origami T15<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>characters / objects<\/td><td>vector-character skill; objects with faces 6.7, speech 6.8<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>kinetic text, transitions, map<\/td><td>Chapters 7, 8, 9<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>voice<\/td><td>Chapter 10<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>checked first<\/td><td>Chapter 12 (review before render)<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Real sample briefs:<\/p>\n<ol><li><strong>Ad.<\/strong> \"I want to make a motion graphics ad for a packaged iced milk coffee, 15 seconds, 9:16, in a warm paper collage style. Show a coffee cup with a smiling face, kinetic text for its three strengths, then a logo reveal at the end. Use a friendly female voice-over.\"<\/li><li><strong>Explainer.<\/strong> \"Make a 60-second 16:9 explainer on how a savings app works, in a flat vector style. A vector character walks while explaining, the transitions between steps have a reason, and a savings chart grows.\"<\/li><li><strong>Documentary.<\/strong> \"I want a 2-minute video essay about the spice route, in a layered paper archive style, with a real map and a route that grows from Maluku to Venice, a calm male narrator, and a check before render.\"<\/li><li><strong>Music video.<\/strong> \"Make a 9:16 lyric music video for my song (lyrics attached), origami style, kinetic typography on the beat, a 3D camera turning slowly, no AI assets.\"<\/li><li><strong>Education.<\/strong> \"Make a 45-second educational video for primary school children about the water cycle, pixel art style. The character is a water drop with a face that can talk, bouncy motion, with a child narrator's voice.\"<\/li><\/ol>\n<p>Tips for the user: if unsure about the style, write \"help me choose a style\"; attach assets when you have them (logo, product photo, lyrics, voice recording); Claude stops at decision points (REQUIRED gates, chapter 1.1) to ask for approval.<\/p>\n<p>Fields that are not mentioned are asked through T1 (2.1); \"no AI assets\" means everything is drawn in code (the branch without Higgsfield, chapter 15).<\/p>\n<h4>17.2 Motion pack library<\/h4>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">17.2<\/span> <strong>Motion pack library<\/strong> (folder <code>packs/<\/code> of this skill). 104 packs in 11 categories: EXP explainer, DOK documentary, NAR animated story, ADS ads, TLK talking head, SHO vertical short, KIN kinetic typography, DAT data/infographic, DEM product demo, EDU education, TRN new trends: pixel and origami (category 11, Version 2.1).<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>File<\/th><th>Contents<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td><code>packs/INDEX.md<\/code><\/td><td>Table of every pack (id, name, category, aspect ratio, duration) + details per pack: use, key motion, palette, fonts, slots, blocks per slot, brief ready to copy<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>packs/packs.json<\/code><\/td><td>The same data for code to read (categories, packs, research sources)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>packs/packs-preview.js<\/code><\/td><td>Preview engine: <code>PaketPreview.build(host, pack, category)<\/code> makes a looping 10 s animation (Web Animations API) with <code>seek/play/pause<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>packs/motion-packs.html<\/code><\/td><td>Offline browser: open it in a browser, filter by category/aspect ratio, search, preview per tile, details in a dialog<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>motion-graphics<\/code> <code>lib/pack-scenes/<\/code> (Version 2.2)<\/td><td>Moving scenes for all 104 packs: <code>MGP.packs.create(target, id, options)<\/code> or <code>MGP.packs.render(id, ctx, t, { width, height })<\/code>, 76 scene types, 8 s loop, the pack's palette/fonts/aspect ratio<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>How to use:<\/p>\n<ol><li>In the concept/taste stage (2.2-2.3) Claude proposes 1 featured pack of that category + 1-2 alternatives; the <code>cocok<\/code> (fits) / <code>hindari<\/code> (avoid) fields are used to filter.<\/li><li>The pack's <code>brief<\/code> field becomes the T1 draft; the slots (style/background, composition, lettering, subtitles, accents, rhythm, transitions, camera, audio, aspect ratio) become the T2/T3 fields.<\/li><li>A pack brief turns straight into a moving picture: <code>MGP.packs.create(\"#stage\", \"DOK-03\", { lines: [{ j: \"Title\", c: \"Caption\" }], ar: \"9:16\" })<\/code>, then <code>MGP.core.expose(p)<\/code> and <code>mgp render<\/code> (11.5); to insert one pack frame into your own canvas: <code>MGP.packs.render(id, ctx, t, { width, height, ar: \"9:16\", lines: [...] })<\/code> (<code>render<\/code> takes the same <code>ar<\/code>, <code>lines<\/code>, <code>pal<\/code>, <code>font<\/code>, <code>scene<\/code>/<code>variant<\/code> options as <code>create<\/code>; without <code>ar<\/code> a 16:9 pack is fitted small into a 9:16 box; reuse one options object per pack across frames; for many packs at once, e.g. thumbnails, add <code>fonts: false<\/code>; details in <code>motion-graphics/lib/README.md<\/code>, Motion packs). The options <code>pal<\/code>, <code>font<\/code>, <code>scene<\/code>/<code>variant<\/code>, <code>duration<\/code>, <code>seam<\/code> adapt it to a brand. Used for style frames and test clips in 2.3, before any other asset.<\/li><li>The <code>add<\/code> field is the block name for that pack: insert it with <code>mgp add &lt;name&gt;<\/code> (own blocks in <code>blocks/<\/code>, 260 blocks; old item names from the HyperFrames format are also accepted and mapped through the <code>aliases<\/code> field); check first with <code>mgp catalog<\/code>.<\/li><li>A pack's palette and fonts are only a starting point; the project's brand/brief still wins.<\/li><\/ol>\n<p>Featured pick per category: EXP-02, DOK-03, NAR-02, ADS-03, TLK-02, SHO-01, KIN-01, DAT-01, DEM-02, EDU-02, TRN-03.<\/p>\n<p>TRN category (Version 2.1):<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pack<\/th><th>Core<\/th><th>Aspect ratio, duration<\/th><th>Palette, fonts<\/th><th>Key motion<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>TRN-01 8-bit Arcade Pixel Art<\/td><td>Sharp pixel grid, limited palette, choppy motion at 10 fps, score HUD, dialogue box<\/td><td>16:9, 15-30 seconds<\/td><td>#0B0A1F #FFFFFF #FF3864 #2DE2E6 #F9C80E; Press Start 2P + Pixelify Sans<\/td><td>Character runs and jumps on every beat collecting coins; layered side-scrolling city and hills; score counts up, dialogue is typed<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>TRN-02 16-bit Cozy Pixel Art<\/td><td>Warm pixel room: morning-dusk-night, rain, cat, dithered lamps<\/td><td>9:16, 20-45 seconds<\/td><td>#2B2140 #FFF4E0 #F4A259 #8CB369 #F7D08A; Pixelify Sans + Inter<\/td><td>Time changes through palette swaps; rain, coffee steam, cat tail in steps; dialogue typed slowly<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>TRN-03 Folding Origami Crane<\/td><td>Paper folded for real step by step into a crane that flaps and then flies<\/td><td>16:9, 15-60 seconds<\/td><td>#F3EDE2 #2A2622 #D1495B #EDAE49 #00798C; Shippori Mincho + Zen Maru Gothic<\/td><td>Two folds with front and back sides in different colours; the crane bounces, then flaps; flies out, paper planes in the background<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>TRN-04 Fold-Unfold Origami<\/td><td>Accordion paper opens to reveal a message, dog-ears as accents, a paper plane carries the call to action<\/td><td>16:9, 15-60 seconds<\/td><td>#1F2A44 #FFF8EE #F25F5C #FFE066 #70C1B3; Fraunces + Inter<\/td><td>Accordion opens fold by fold; folded card corner + crease sheen; a paper plane carries the CTA<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>TRN audio: chiptune 140-150 BPM + coin/jump SFX (TRN-01); lo-fi 70-85 BPM + rain/typing (TRN-02); piano/koto 70-90 BPM + paper rustle (TRN-03); light acoustic 90-100 BPM + paper folding (TRN-04).<\/p>\n<h4>17.3 Pixel art and origami<\/h4>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">17.3<\/span> <strong>Pixel art and origami in the production flow.<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol><li>When the brief asks for this style, T2 uses T14 (pixel art) or T15 (origami) as the rule sheet and a TRN pack as the starting point; its rules are confirmed at G2 like any other style.<\/li><li>Pixel art is drawn in code on a small canvas, then enlarged with an integer scale (nearest neighbour); every pixel is locked to a fixed palette; 1-pixel outlines. Motion at 8-12 drawings per second, stepped. Motion and character recipes: the <code>motion-graphics<\/code> skill chapter 12.5 (<code>motion-graphics/references/scene-recipes.md<\/code>) and the <code>vector-character<\/code> skill chapter 19 (<code>vector-character/references/style.md<\/code>).<\/li><li>Origami is built from triangle/kite planes with a light and a dark side per fold and a cream back side; motion at 12 drawings per second; transitions = fold/unfold. Recipes: <code>motion-graphics<\/code> 12.5 (<code>motion-graphics/references/scene-recipes.md<\/code>), <code>vector-character<\/code> 19 (<code>vector-character/references/style.md<\/code>).<\/li><li>Extra QC: pixel art with no mixed/half pixels, no extra palette colours, the camera on whole pixels; origami without thick ink lines, every plane has a light-dark pair, the back side shows when a plane turns over.<\/li><\/ol>\n<h4>17.4 Asset trick: flat magenta background + local key<\/h4>\n<p class=\"rule\"><span class=\"rn\">17.4<\/span> <strong>Higgsfield asset trick: flat magenta background + local key<\/strong> (proven for buildings and ornaments with openings).<\/p>\n<ol><li><strong>Prompt.<\/strong> A single asset (object, gate, ornament, character) is requested on a flat magenta background: \"isolated on a flat solid pure magenta #FF00FF background, no texture, no gradient, no shadow on the background\". Every opening is requested in magenta too: \"every opening, window, arch and lattice gap shows the same flat magenta #FF00FF\". So that nothing is cut off: \"the whole object fully visible with a wide margin on every side, nothing touching or cropped at the edges\".<\/li><li><strong>Why magenta.<\/strong> This colour hardly occurs in paper, wood, stone, skin or leaves, so the colour key is clean; lattice gaps and windows become transparent too, without flood fill and without reopening holes (compare 5.2). A white background is still used for kits with many pieces (5.1). Avoid objects with strong magenta/purple colours; if there are any, check that part on the contact sheet.<\/li><li><strong>Local key<\/strong> (<code>scripts/magenta_key.py<\/code>, logic from the script used in production):<ul><li>m = min(R, B) - G (magenta amount); sat = max(R, B) - min(R, B).<\/li><li>alpha = clip(1 - (m - 40) / 70, 0, 1): m &gt;= 110 transparent, m &lt;= 40 opaque.<\/li><li>sat &gt; 90 (strong red or strong blue, not both) stays opaque.<\/li><li>Dark magenta (background in shadow): G &lt; 0.3 x min(R, B) and min(R, B) &gt; 45 becomes transparent.<\/li><li>Despill: spill = max(m - 10, 0) on semi-transparent pixels (half of it on opaque pixels with m &gt; 25); R and B are reduced by 0.8 x spill.<\/li><li>Alpha: MinFilter 3 (erode 1 px), then Gaussian 0.7; save an RGBA PNG + a preview on dark blue (20, 30, 60) for QC.<\/li><\/ul><\/li><\/ol>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>python -I scripts/magenta_key.py assets/raw/gate.png assets/src/gate.png --preview review/k_gate.jpg<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<ol><li><strong>QC<\/strong> (5.4): preview on a dark background; lattice gaps really see-through; no pink fringe; edges not cut off (if cut off, generate again with a wider margin, do not patch). Job IDs, credits and the key result are recorded in T5.<\/li><\/ol>"},{"n":18,"title":"Machine: check what the computer can do, then pick a route (v2.4)","html":"<p>Reference for Claude. The goal: do not guess what the user's computer can do. Measure first, then pick the voice, image and render routes from those measurements.<\/p>\n<h4>1. At the start of a project: run <code>mgp doctor<\/code><\/h4>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>node motion-graphics-pro/render/bin/mgp.mjs doctor          # short summary (English; MGP_LANG=id for Indonesian)\nnode motion-graphics-pro/render/bin/mgp.mjs doctor --json   # for Claude to read<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Run it once at the start of a project (before the brief is finished), and again when the user says they are on a different computer, has just installed a driver/PyTorch CUDA, or their RAM is currently full. The voice venv and the image venv are found automatically (env <code>MGP_PYTHON<\/code> / <code>MGP_IMAGE_PYTHON<\/code>, then <code>~/.mgp/install.json<\/code> from <code>mgp install<\/code>, then the old manual venvs <code>~/vsu<\/code> / <code>~/vgb<\/code>); the <code>Install<\/code> line in the output sums up the install status.<\/p>\n<h5>Start the install in the background right away<\/h5>\n<p>When <code>doctor<\/code> shows that a local engine the project will use is not installed yet (<code>Install  no install.json yet<\/code>, a note \"mgp install ... --detach\") and the user agrees to download several GB:<\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>node motion-graphics-pro/render/bin/mgp.mjs install all --dry-run   # show first: PyTorch build, device, disk (±9 GB voice, ±5 GB image)\nnode motion-graphics-pro/render/bin/mgp.mjs install all --detach    # runs in the background; carry on with the brief with the user\nnode motion-graphics-pro/render/bin/mgp.mjs install all --status      # check progress at any time (downloading x%, installing, done, failed)<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<ul><li>Pick the target the project needs: <code>voice<\/code> (local clone), <code>image<\/code> (local FLUX), or <code>all<\/code>. Do not install local image generation when <code>doctor<\/code> advises the cloud (total RAM &lt; 16 GB).<\/li><li>When <code>--status<\/code> reports \"a complete manual/env install was found\", do not download again: <code>mgp install all --adopt<\/code> (sha256 is checked, then the smoke test runs).<\/li><li>An interrupted install (network, laptop switched off): run the same command again; downloads resume where they stopped. <code>mgp install --stop<\/code> stops the background process.<\/li><li>Done = the smoke test passed (1 sentence with the built-in voice / 1 image of 512 px; device + time are recorded in <code>--status<\/code>). An image test that is PENDING because of too little free RAM is not an install failure.<\/li><li>Only one install at a time; the smoke test uses the GPU through the shared lock (section 3), so it does not collide with other render/voice jobs.<\/li><\/ul>\n<p>What is reported: CPU and thread count, total/free RAM, every GPU (total/free VRAM, driver), the ffmpeg hardware video encoders that <strong>actually pass an encode test<\/strong>, the PyTorch build (cpu/cuda/mps) and ctranslate2, GPU lock status, then advice per pipeline. The thresholds are in one table: <code>render/py/hardware-requirements.json<\/code>.<\/p>\n<h4>2. Use the results at the decision points<\/h4>\n<p>Local engines are installed <strong>once per computer<\/strong>, not per project: <code>mgp install voice<\/code> (voice clone) and <code>mgp install image<\/code> (local images). The command detects OS/GPU/RAM, picks the PyTorch build (CUDA/MPS/CPU), creates a venv on a short path, downloads version-locked models (sha256, resumable; the download may run in the background), then tests one sentence or one image. Models are not in the pack ZIP. Offer it at the start of a project when <code>doctor<\/code> shows a local engine is not installed yet; never install without the user's permission (a download of several GB).<\/p>\n<p>The image venv is checked separately from the voice venv (the <code>Image<\/code> line in the output), so the local image advice no longer wrongly says \"needs PyTorch CUDA\" when the image venv is ready.<\/p>\n<h5>Voice (script/VO gate)<\/h5>\n<p>Read <code>recommendation.voice<\/code> in the JSON.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Condition<\/th><th>What Claude offers<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td><code>weak: false<\/code>, GPU device<\/td><td>Local clone as the main, free option. Mention the estimated <code>secondsPer30sVo<\/code>.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>weak: false<\/code>, CPU device<\/td><td>Local clone still works, but mention the time (e.g. \"30 s of VO takes ±2.5 minutes\"). For scripts &gt; 1 minute suggest making it per block.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>weak: true<\/code> (high RTF or too little free RAM)<\/td><td>Prefer ElevenLabs or the user's own voice recording. Local clone only when the user still wants it.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>note \"PyTorch CPU build\" while an NVIDIA GPU is present<\/td><td>Tell the user that installing PyTorch CUDA speeds up the clone (see <code>voice/README.md<\/code>). Do not install without permission: the download is ±2.5 GB.<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>The RTF number comes from the latest measurement on that machine (<code>ukur.json<\/code>, written automatically on every <code>mgp voice speak<\/code>); when nothing has been measured yet, the table default is used and the JSON says <code>rtfSource: \"estimate\"<\/code>.<\/p>\n<h5>Local images (<code>mgp image<\/code>, when available)<\/h5>\n<p><code>recommendation.image.mode<\/code>:<\/p>\n<ul><li><code>local<\/code>: large GPU (VRAM ≥ 12 GB), local images are reasonable.<\/li><li><code>local-offload<\/code>: VRAM ≥ 4 GB and free RAM ≥ 16 GB. Possible but slow; use it for a few images (kit sheets, plates), not dozens.<\/li><li><code>local-mps<\/code>: Apple Silicon with plenty of RAM; slow.<\/li><li><code>cloud<\/code>: use the free cloud (<code>mgp image --source free-cloud<\/code>, <code>references/image.md<\/code>) or Higgsfield (gate G3). This is the default answer for an ordinary laptop. When the notes say free RAM is too low although total RAM is large, you may suggest closing other apps and checking again.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h5>Render<\/h5>\n<ul><li><code>--workers<\/code>: use <code>recommendation.render.workers<\/code> when the computer is used only for this; use <code>workersShared<\/code> (2) when the user is running other sessions/agents. When the user runs other sessions or agents on the same laptop, always use <code>--workers 2<\/code>.<\/li><li>Encoder: the <strong>final<\/strong> render uses <code>--encoder cpu<\/code> (libx264, default, deterministic). <code>--encoder auto<\/code> or a hardware encoder that passed the test (<code>encoder.draft<\/code>) is only for drafts/iterations; in ordinary compositions the render time is dominated by Chrome frame capture, so a hardware encoder hardly speeds up the total (see the numbers in <code>render/README.md<\/code>).<\/li><li><code>smallPreview: true<\/code>: drafts at half <code>--size<\/code> (e.g. 540x960), the final at full size.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>3. The GPU is used in turns<\/h4>\n<p>Every mgp GPU job (voice clone, faster-whisper in <code>mgp voice score<\/code>, <code>mgp image<\/code>) takes the lock <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\\rekam\\gpu.lock<\/code> (Linux/macOS: <code>~/.cache/rekam/gpu.lock<\/code>; the folder keeps the old name so old and new versions share one queue). One GPU job at a time, across processes, agents and sessions. A job that comes later waits <code>MGP_GPU_WAIT<\/code> seconds (default 120), then runs on the CPU and writes that in the log. A lock left by a process that has died is removed automatically.<\/p>\n<p>Rules for Claude:<\/p>\n<ul><li>Do not run two heavy GPU jobs at the same time from two agents; let the lock handle it, and never delete a <code>gpu.lock<\/code> that belongs to a process that is still alive.<\/li><li>Every voice command prints a line <code>device [task]: GPU (CUDA): ...<\/code> or <code>CPU: &lt;reason&gt;<\/code>. Report this line to the user when the VO feels slow.<\/li><li>When the log says \"fallback after CUDA out of memory\", the result is still valid (redone on the CPU), only slower. Common cause: another app is using VRAM. No need to redo it.<\/li><li>NVENC/QSV encoders do not take the lock (the encoder chip is separate and uses little VRAM); NVENC falls back to the CPU when VRAM is nearly full.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>4. Environment variables<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Env<\/th><th>Meaning<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td><code>MGP_DEVICE=auto|cpu|cuda|mps<\/code><\/td><td>Force the device of the Python models (default <code>auto<\/code>).<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>MGP_GPU_WAIT=&lt;seconds&gt;<\/code><\/td><td>How long to wait for a busy GPU before falling back to the CPU (default 120).<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>MGP_GPU_LOCK=&lt;file&gt;<\/code><\/td><td>Another lock file location (rarely needed).<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>MGP_ENCODER=cpu|auto|nvenc|qsv|amf|videotoolbox<\/code><\/td><td>Default MP4 encoder for <code>mgp render<\/code> (default <code>cpu<\/code>).<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>MGP_PYTHON=&lt;python.exe&gt;<\/code><\/td><td>Python with PyTorch/the voice engine (default: from <code>install.json<\/code>).<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>MGP_IMAGE_PYTHON=&lt;python.exe&gt;<\/code><\/td><td>Python of the image venv (default: from <code>install.json<\/code>, then <code>~/vgb<\/code>).<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>MGP_HOME=&lt;folder&gt;<\/code><\/td><td>Home of <code>mgp install<\/code> (default <code>~/.mgp<\/code>; an old <code>~/.rekam<\/code> is still read): <code>install.json<\/code>, background status/log, venvs, models.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>MGP_CHATTERBOX_DIR<\/code>, <code>MGP_OPENVOICE_DIR<\/code>, <code>MGP_WHISPER_CACHE<\/code>, <code>MGP_IMAGE_MODEL<\/code><\/td><td>Model folders; filled in automatically from <code>install.json<\/code> when empty.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>MGP_GPU_LIMIT=&lt;GB&gt;<\/code><\/td><td>Testing only: limit the CUDA memory of the process to trigger the OOM fallback route.<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<h4>5. For new Python scripts (e.g. <code>mgp image<\/code>)<\/h4>\n<p>Use <code>render/py/device.py<\/code>; do not write your own device logic:<\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>import os, sys\nsys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))   # the script runs with python -I\nimport device as pk\n\ndef work(k):                        # k.device: &quot;cuda&quot; | &quot;cuda+offload&quot; | &quot;cpu&quot; | &quot;mps&quot;; k.torch for .to()\n    pipe = load_pipeline(k.torch)\n    if k.device == &quot;cuda+offload&quot;:\n        pipe.enable_model_cpu_offload()\n    return make_image(pipe)\n\ndef drop():                         # called before the CPU retry after an OOM\n    global PIPE; PIPE = None\n\nresult, k = pk.run(&quot;image flux&quot;, work, on_fallback=drop, task=&quot;flux-schnell&quot;)\nprint(k.text())                     # &quot;GPU + RAM offload: free VRAM 3.8 GB &lt; needed 12.0 GB; ...&quot;<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Add new task rows (VRAM, RAM, offload) to <code>render/py/hardware-requirements.json<\/code> after measuring, not in the code.<\/p>"},{"n":19,"title":"`project.json`: one source of project data (v2.4)","html":"<p><code>project.json<\/code> sits at the root of the project folder, next to <code>index.html<\/code>. It holds the data that used to be spread over T3, T5, T6, and chat notes. The composition stays the source of truth for the render; <code>project.json<\/code> is a table of contents that a machine can check. Claude updates it in the same turn as the change to the files.<\/p>\n<h4>Schema (version 1)<\/h4>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>{\n  &quot;version&quot;: 1,\n  &quot;meta&quot;: { &quot;title&quot;: &quot;30 s ad&quot;, &quot;aspect&quot;: &quot;9:16&quot;, &quot;width&quot;: 1080, &quot;height&quot;: 1920, &quot;fps&quot;: 24, &quot;duration&quot;: 30,\n            &quot;created&quot;: &quot;2026-11-05&quot;, &quot;skill&quot;: &quot;motion-graphics-pro 2.4&quot;, &quot;stage&quot;: &quot;7 Pilot&quot;, &quot;gate&quot;: &quot;G4a pending&quot; },\n  &quot;composition&quot;: &quot;index.html&quot;,\n  &quot;voice&quot;: {\n    &quot;source&quot;: &quot;local-clone&quot;,\n    &quot;profile&quot;: &quot;narrator&quot;,\n    &quot;vo&quot;: [ { &quot;id&quot;: &quot;vo&quot;, &quot;file&quot;: &quot;assets/audio/vo.wav&quot;, &quot;script&quot;: &quot;script.txt&quot;,\n              &quot;words&quot;: &quot;assets/audio/vo.words.json&quot;, &quot;letters&quot;: &quot;assets/audio/vo.letters.json&quot;, &quot;start&quot;: 0.4 } ]\n  },\n  &quot;scenes&quot;: [\n    { &quot;id&quot;: &quot;s01&quot;, &quot;start&quot;: 0, &quot;duration&quot;: 5.2, &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Hook&quot;, &quot;branch&quot;: &quot;A&quot;,\n      &quot;composition&quot;: &quot;compositions/s01.html&quot;, &quot;poses&quot;: [0, 1.0, 2.5, 4.5], &quot;status&quot;: &quot;blocking&quot;,\n      &quot;blocks&quot;: [&quot;text/highlighter&quot;] }\n  ],\n  &quot;blocks&quot;: [ { &quot;id&quot;: &quot;transitions/tear&quot;, &quot;scene&quot;: &quot;s02&quot;, &quot;start&quot;: 4.7 } ],\n  &quot;caption&quot;: { &quot;file&quot;: &quot;assets/audio/vo.words.json&quot;, &quot;style&quot;: &quot;cream strip 3-5 words&quot; },\n  &quot;sfx&quot;: &quot;assets/audio/sfx.json&quot;,\n  &quot;assets&quot;: [\n    { &quot;file&quot;: &quot;assets/pieces/k1/cloud1.png&quot;, &quot;source&quot;: &quot;higgsfield&quot;, &quot;job&quot;: &quot;&lt;job id&gt;&quot;, &quot;license&quot;: &quot;user account&quot; },\n    { &quot;file&quot;: &quot;assets/kit/character/kit.json&quot;, &quot;source&quot;: &quot;mgp-image&quot;, &quot;provider&quot;: &quot;local&quot;, &quot;model&quot;: &quot;flux2-klein-4b&quot;,\n      &quot;seed&quot;: 7, &quot;license&quot;: &quot;Apache-2.0&quot;, &quot;meta&quot;: &quot;assets/raw/character_sheet.json&quot; }\n  ]\n}<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Key<\/th><th>Contents<\/th><th>Used by<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td><code>meta<\/code><\/td><td><code>title<\/code>, <code>aspect<\/code>, <code>width<\/code>/<code>height<\/code>, <code>fps<\/code>, <code>duration<\/code>, <code>created<\/code> (date), last <code>stage<\/code> and <code>gate<\/code><\/td><td><code>mgp check<\/code> (compared with the composition), T10<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>composition<\/code><\/td><td>root file<\/td><td><code>mgp check<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>voice.source<\/code><\/td><td><code>pending<\/code> / <code>elevenlabs<\/code> / <code>local-clone<\/code> / <code>recording<\/code> / <code>none<\/code> (decision point G2, <code>references/audio.md<\/code> 10.1)<\/td><td><code>mgp check<\/code> reminds you while it is still <code>pending<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>voice.vo[]<\/code><\/td><td>VO file, script (<code>script<\/code>), per-word timing (<code>words<\/code>, <code>*.words.json<\/code>) and per-letter timing (<code>letters<\/code>, <code>*.letters.json<\/code>), start second in the video (<code>start<\/code>)<\/td><td><code>mgp check<\/code>, lip-sync (<code>references/lipsync.md<\/code>), captions<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>scenes[]<\/code><\/td><td><code>id<\/code>, <code>start<\/code> + <code>duration<\/code> (seconds), <code>title<\/code>, <code>branch<\/code> A/B/C, <code>composition<\/code> file (for a sub-composition), <code>poses<\/code> (key poses, seconds relative to the scene), <code>status<\/code> (<code>blocking<\/code> / <code>spline<\/code> / <code>locked<\/code>), <code>blocks<\/code> in that scene<\/td><td><code>mgp contact --times poses<\/code>, <code>mgp check<\/code>, T3<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>blocks[]<\/code><\/td><td>inserted blocks (<code>mgp add<\/code>), <code>scene<\/code>, <code>start<\/code> second<\/td><td><code>mgp check<\/code> (the block must exist in <code>blocks/<\/code>)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>caption<\/code><\/td><td>word timing source (<code>file<\/code>) + <code>style<\/code><\/td><td>typography (<code>references/typography.md<\/code>)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>sfx<\/code><\/td><td>cues file for <code>mgp sfx mix<\/code><\/td><td>audio<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><code>assets[]<\/code><\/td><td>every asset from outside the code, with its <strong>provenance<\/strong>: <code>source<\/code> (<code>higgsfield<\/code> / <code>mgp-image<\/code> / <code>user<\/code> / <code>code<\/code> / <code>other<\/code>), <code>provider<\/code>, <code>model<\/code>, <code>job<\/code>, <code>seed<\/code>, <code>license<\/code>, <code>meta<\/code> (the JSON next to a PNG from <code>mgp image<\/code>), <code>notes<\/code><\/td><td><code>mgp check<\/code> (file exists, <code>source<\/code> filled in), T5 log<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p><code>meta.gate<\/code> is a short text: the gate id, then a status word, optionally who and when, e.g. <code>\"G4 approved (reviewer) 2026-11-05\"<\/code> or <code>\"G4 disetujui (peninjau)\"<\/code>. <code>mgp audit<\/code> reads the status words in English and Indonesian (case does not matter):<\/p>\n<ul><li>approved: <code>approved<\/code>, <code>passed<\/code>, <code>pass<\/code>, <code>done<\/code>, <code>ok<\/code>, <code>okay<\/code>, <code>accepted<\/code>, <code>signed off<\/code>, <code>locked<\/code>, <code>complete<\/code>, <code>completed<\/code>, <code>disetujui<\/code>, <code>setuju<\/code>, <code>lolos<\/code>, <code>lulus<\/code>, <code>selesai<\/code>, <code>beres<\/code>, <code>oke<\/code>, <code>acc<\/code>, <code>diterima<\/code>, <code>terkunci<\/code>, <code>✓<\/code>;<\/li><li>still open: <code>pending<\/code>, <code>waiting<\/code>, <code>open<\/code>, <code>todo<\/code>, <code>draft<\/code>, <code>rejected<\/code>, <code>not approved<\/code>, <code>not passed<\/code>, <code>revise<\/code>, <code>revision<\/code>, <code>changes requested<\/code>, <code>on hold<\/code>, <code>menunggu<\/code>, <code>belum<\/code>, <code>ditolak<\/code>, <code>revisi<\/code>, <code>tunda<\/code>, <code>ditunda<\/code>, <code>dalam proses<\/code>. An open word wins (<code>\"G4 belum disetujui\"<\/code> is open).<\/li><\/ul>\n<p>A gate id without a status word (<code>\"G4 (peninjau)\"<\/code>) gives a WARN that lists these words. A gate number above the one the stage needs counts as passed for that stage (stage 8 needs G4, stage 9 needs G5).<\/p>\n<p>Rules:<\/p>\n<ul><li>Every path is relative to the folder of <code>project.json<\/code>. <code>http(s)<\/code> URLs are skipped by the check.<\/li><li>Extra keys may be written (e.g. <code>motion_library<\/code>, <code>palette<\/code>); the checker ignores unknown keys.<\/li><li>Old files are still read: <code>proyek.json<\/code> (old file name) and the old Indonesian keys and values (<code>versi<\/code>, <code>adegan<\/code>, <code>kunci<\/code>, <code>klon-lokal<\/code>, ...). <code>mgp new<\/code> writes the English keys.<\/li><li>The user's own voice recording is NOT copied into the project folder. <code>voice.profile<\/code> is only the name of a local profile.<\/li><li><code>mgp check index.html<\/code> reads <code>project.json<\/code> in the composition folder (or one level above it) and gives a warning (not an error) for: a referenced file that does not exist, an unknown block, an asset without <code>source<\/code>, fps/size/duration different from the composition, a scene or key pose outside the duration, voice source not chosen yet.<\/li><\/ul>"}],"templates":[{"id":"T1","title":"Brief","html":"<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field<\/th><th>Answer<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Title; goal and main message<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Platform, aspect, duration<\/td><td>&lt;Reels/TikTok/Shorts&gt;, &lt;9:16/16:9&gt;, &lt;s&gt; (platform limits checked &lt;date&gt;)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Audience<\/td><td>&lt;age, viewing context, language&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>VO; music and SFX<\/td><td>&lt;voice, language&gt;; &lt;music yes/no, SFX style&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>References<\/td><td>Likes / dislikes: &lt;link + reason&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Content rules<\/td><td>&lt;forbidden figures, how to depict them, required sources&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Concept; personification<\/td><td>&lt;types liked/avoided&gt;; &lt;used or not, which object&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Brand, palette, fonts; assets<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;; &lt;old jobs, logo, files&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Deadline; credit budget; output<\/td><td>&lt;date&gt;; Higgsfield/ElevenLabs &lt;credits&gt;; &lt;resolution&gt;, 24 fps (frame choice: chapter 2.0)<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>"},{"id":"T2","title":"Style comparison + element confirmation","html":"<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Aspect<\/th><th>Recommendation: &lt;style A&gt;<\/th><th>Option B: &lt;style&gt;<\/th><th>Option C: &lt;style&gt;<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Palette (hex + share)<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Material, shapes, typography<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Motion, transitions<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Strengths / weaknesses<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Fits; visual sample<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;; &lt;file&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;; &lt;file&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;; &lt;file&gt;<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Element<\/th><th>Proposal<\/th><th>OK/revise<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Palette; typography<\/td><td>&lt;hex + share&gt;; &lt;font, size, frame&gt;<\/td><td><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Drawing style; characters<\/td><td>&lt;kit, edges, shadows&gt;; &lt;proportions, sequence&gt;<\/td><td><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Motion; transitions; camera<\/td><td>24 fps, on twos, entrance 0.17 s; &lt;per intent&gt;; &lt;push/pull, k&gt;<\/td><td><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Effects; format; audio<\/td><td>&lt;grain, light&gt;; &lt;aspect ratio, safe zone&gt;; &lt;VO, SFX, loudness&gt;<\/td><td><\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>"},{"id":"T2a","title":"Concepts (3 per video, side by side)","html":"<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Aspect<\/th><th>Recommendation: &lt;concept A&gt;<\/th><th>Option B: &lt;concept&gt;<\/th><th>Option C: &lt;concept&gt;<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Type (max 2, chapter 2.2)<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Meaning-carrying object; personification<\/td><td>&lt;object&gt;; &lt;yes: which object / no&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Frame 0 hook<\/td><td>&lt;what shows on frame 0&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Flow of 4-6 scenes<\/td><td>1 &lt;...&gt;; 2 &lt;...&gt;; 3 &lt;...&gt;; 4 &lt;...&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Proving motion<\/td><td>&lt;the relationship that changes&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Final scene payoff<\/td><td>&lt;the meaning-carrying object returns&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Asset branch; motion library<\/td><td>&lt;A/B/C per scene&gt;; &lt;actions reused&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Credit estimate<\/td><td>&lt;count from chapter 15&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Content-rule risk<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Strengths / weaknesses<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;fill in&gt;<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>After the table: the good and bad of each concept in 1-2 sentences, then the G1 question in chat + interactive.<\/p>"},{"id":"T3","title":"Shot list / beat sheet","html":"<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>No<\/th><th>Seconds<\/th><th>Trigger VO word<\/th><th>Set<\/th><th>Branch<\/th><th>POV<\/th><th>Hero<\/th><th>Action<\/th><th>Asset<\/th><th>Entry transition<\/th><th>Text<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>1<\/td><td>0.00-2.40<\/td><td>\"&lt;word&gt;\" (1.20)<\/td><td>&lt;kit&gt;<\/td><td>A/B/C<\/td><td>Observer<\/td><td>&lt;hero&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;action + seconds&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;job code&gt;<\/td><td>Cut (hook)<\/td><td>&lt;kicker / headline / keyword&gt;<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>"},{"id":"T4","title":"Higgsfield prompts","html":"<p>Order of every prompt: CANVAS, content (PIECES/SET/CHARACTER/ROW/PEOPLE), STYLE (block 4.3, which already forbids text/logos), ONLY. White pieces use \"flat plain mid-blue background\".<\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>(a) KIT 21:9\nCANVAS: Wide 21:9 sheet on a flat plain pure white background. &lt;N&gt; separate cut-paper pieces for one location (&lt;location, era&gt;) in &lt;2-3&gt; loose rows, white space of at least one tenth of the sheet width between pieces, each piece complete and not touching another, all at one consistent scale.\nPIECES: 1. &lt;piece, size relative to an adult, view, facing frame left/right&gt;. 2. &lt;piece&gt;.\nONLY: Show only the listed pieces on the plain background; add nothing else.\n\n(b) PLATE 9:16\nCANVAS: Vertical 9:16 scene, &lt;eye-level/top-down&gt;, one-point perspective, vanishing point at the horizontal centre about &lt;45&gt; percent from the top. Empty scene: no people. Keep &lt;the lower third&gt; clear and low-detail for pieces added later.\nSET: &lt;location, time, light from frame left/right, fixed objects and their positions&gt;.\nONLY: Show only what this prompt describes; add nothing else.\n\n(c) SEQUENCE 6 FRAME\nCANVAS: Wide 21:9 sheet on a flat plain pure white background. Six full-body frames of the same character in one horizontal row, six equal columns, identical size, feet on one shared baseline, a white gutter of at least a quarter column between figures.\nCHARACTER: &lt;identical description: age, body, hair, clothing and colours, shoes, accessories&gt;. Paper collage figure with natural human proportions; each limb is one smooth continuous paper shape. &lt;Smooth blank face.&gt;\nFRAMES (side view, facing frame right; every body part changes in every frame): F1 contact, front heel down, rear toe pushing off; F2 down, weight on the front leg, knee bent; F3 passing, rear leg swinging past the standing leg; F4 contact, other leg in front; F5 down; F6 passing. Arms swing opposite to the legs; head and hair follow one beat later.\nONLY: Show only the six figures on the white background; add nothing else.\nWave (front view): F1 arm relaxed; F2 lifting, elbow bent; F3 hand at shoulder, palm out; F4 above head tilted to frame left; F5 upright; F6 tilted to frame right.\n\n(d) FACE KIT\nCANVAS: Wide 21:9 sheet on a flat plain pure white background, two rows (three rows for a face-bearing object), one scale for a face &lt;width&gt; wide, generous white space.\nROW 1: eight cut-paper mouths in &lt;lip colour&gt;: closed relaxed; wide open showing upper teeth; medium open, lips stretched; rounded open; small tight rounded; lips pressed together; lower lip under the upper front teeth; closed gentle smile.\nROW 2: six pairs of cut-paper eyebrows in &lt;hair colour&gt;: neutral; raised; frowning; worried; angry; happy.\nROW 3 (face-bearing object only): six pairs of cut-paper eyes: open; closed as a curved line; wide open; half-lidded; looking frame left; looking frame right.\nONLY: Show only the pieces listed in the rows above on the white background; add nothing else.\n\n(e) CROWD TWO-STATE\nCANVAS: Wide 21:9 sheet on a flat plain pure white background. &lt;N&gt; people in one row; each person twice side by side, calm on the left and reacting on the right; identical size, feet on one shared baseline, a white gap between people.\nPEOPLE: 1. &lt;age, clothing, head covering, colours, facing frame left/right&gt;; calm: &lt;pose&gt;; reacting: &lt;surprised, hands raised, leaning back&gt;. 2. &lt;next person, complete&gt;.\nONLY: Show only these figures on the white background; add nothing else.\n\n(f) MODEL SHEET 21:9\nCANVAS: Wide 21:9 sheet on a flat plain pure white background. Left two thirds: the same character in four full-body views in one row (front, three-quarter facing frame right, side facing frame right, back), identical scale, feet on one shared baseline, white gutters between views. Right third: three close-ups at a larger scale: &lt;head and face&gt;, &lt;hands&gt;, &lt;costume detail or signature prop&gt;.\nCHARACTER: &lt;identical description, 4.4 item 2&gt;. Face-bearing object: &lt;object&gt;, plain with no face.\nONLY: Show only these views and close-ups on the white background; add nothing else.<\/code><\/pre><\/div>"},{"id":"T5","title":"Higgsfield job log","html":"<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>No<\/th><th>Content<\/th><th>Aspect<\/th><th>Resolution<\/th><th>Job ID<\/th><th>Credits<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>K1<\/td><td>Kit &lt;location&gt;, &lt;N&gt; pieces<\/td><td>21:9<\/td><td>2K<\/td><td>&lt;id&gt;<\/td><td>2.75<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>Batch credits: &lt;X&gt;. Session total: &lt;Y&gt;. Balance before &lt;A&gt;, after &lt;B&gt; (tool <code>balance<\/code>, &lt;date time&gt;). Reference: K1 from &lt;style anchor job&gt;. A 429 response = \"failed, no credits used, resent\".<\/p>"},{"id":"T6","title":"Scene assembly data (JSON)","html":"<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>{\n  &quot;id&quot;: &quot;s03&quot;, &quot;dur&quot;: 5.2, &quot;pivot&quot;: [360, 760], &quot;pov&quot;: &quot;observer&quot;,\n  &quot;camera&quot;: [{&quot;t&quot;: 0, &quot;z&quot;: 1.0, &quot;x&quot;: -14}, {&quot;t&quot;: 2.4, &quot;z&quot;: 1.05, &quot;x&quot;: 14}, {&quot;t&quot;: 5.2, &quot;z&quot;: 1.05, &quot;x&quot;: 14}],\n  &quot;meta&quot;: {&quot;cloud1&quot;: {&quot;w&quot;: 412, &quot;h&quot;: 150}, &quot;walk1&quot;: {&quot;w&quot;: 479, &quot;h&quot;: 880, &quot;anchor&quot;: [246.9, 879]}},\n  &quot;layers&quot;: [\n    {&quot;id&quot;: &quot;cloud1&quot;, &quot;cx&quot;: 250, &quot;bottom&quot;: 182, &quot;scale&quot;: 0.8, &quot;k&quot;: 0.15, &quot;t0&quot;: 0.25, &quot;from&quot;: &quot;r&quot;, &quot;off&quot;: 360, &quot;boil&quot;: 0.7},\n    {&quot;id&quot;: &quot;building&quot;, &quot;cx&quot;: 362, &quot;bottom&quot;: 1015, &quot;scale&quot;: 0.86, &quot;k&quot;: 0.70, &quot;t0&quot;: 1.80, &quot;from&quot;: &quot;pop&quot;, &quot;boil&quot;: 0.7},\n    {&quot;id&quot;: &quot;hero&quot;, &quot;k&quot;: 0.95, &quot;t0&quot;: 0.0, &quot;fade&quot;: 0, &quot;scale&quot;: 0.842, &quot;boil&quot;: 1.0,\n     &quot;seq&quot;: {&quot;intro&quot;: [], &quot;loop&quot;: [&quot;walk1&quot;, &quot;walk2&quot;, &quot;walk3&quot;, &quot;walk4&quot;, &quot;walk5&quot;, &quot;walk6&quot;]},\n     &quot;keys&quot;: [{&quot;t&quot;: 0, &quot;cx&quot;: -60, &quot;bottom&quot;: 1165}, {&quot;t&quot;: 1.83, &quot;cx&quot;: 360, &quot;bottom&quot;: 1165}]}\n  ],\n  &quot;texts&quot;: [{&quot;t0&quot;: 3.0, &quot;t1&quot;: 5.0, &quot;words&quot;: [{&quot;id&quot;: &quot;s03-w0&quot;, &quot;t&quot;: 3.3, &quot;key&quot;: true, &quot;hl&quot;: &quot;s03-hl0&quot;}]}],\n  &quot;sfx&quot;: [{&quot;id&quot;: &quot;N03&quot;, &quot;t&quot;: 0.88}],\n  &quot;trIn&quot;: {&quot;type&quot;: &quot;tear-wipe&quot;, &quot;dur&quot;: 0.5}, &quot;trOut&quot;: {&quot;type&quot;: &quot;cut&quot;, &quot;dur&quot;: 0},\n  &quot;sources&quot;: []\n}<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Fields: <code>meta<\/code> = w, h (+ anchor) for EVERY sprite used; <code>cx<\/code>, <code>bottom<\/code> = the bottom-centre point in the 720 w world (sequences use <code>meta.anchor<\/code>); <code>from<\/code> = b, t, l, r, pop, or empty; <code>fade<\/code> defaults to 0.2 s; <code>seq.intro<\/code> plays once, then <code>seq.loop<\/code> repeats (12 drawings/s); <code>words[].t<\/code> = transcript timestamp; <code>sources<\/code> = sources for numbers, coordinates and proof texts. Branch B layers already matched to the full frame are written with <code>\"full\": true<\/code> (drawn at 0,0 at 720 x 1280). A face-bearing object uses <code>\"face\": {\"anchor\": [x, y], \"w\": &lt;face width&gt;, \"mood\": [[t, \"&lt;state&gt;\"]], \"vis\": \"visemes/&lt;block&gt;_&lt;character&gt;.json\"}<\/code> (6.7, 6.8). The ids and values in this sample (<code>observer<\/code>, <code>cloud1<\/code>, <code>building<\/code>, <code>hero<\/code>, <code>walk1<\/code>-<code>walk6<\/code>, <code>tear-wipe<\/code>) are example project data, not API names.<\/p>"},{"id":"T7","title":"mgp composition skeleton (HyperFrames-compatible format; `mgp new` writes a one-scene version)","html":"<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>&lt;!-- index.html: scene hosts + audio only --&gt;\n&lt;!doctype html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;&lt;body&gt;\n&lt;div id=&quot;root&quot; data-composition-id=&quot;main&quot; data-start=&quot;0&quot; data-duration=&quot;&lt;total&gt;&quot;\n  data-fps=&quot;24&quot; data-width=&quot;1080&quot; data-height=&quot;1920&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;div id=&quot;s01&quot; data-composition-id=&quot;s01&quot; data-composition-src=&quot;compositions/s01.html&quot;\n    data-start=&quot;0&quot; data-duration=&quot;&lt;d1&gt;&quot; data-track-index=&quot;1&quot; data-track-kind=&quot;graphics&quot;\n    data-width=&quot;1080&quot; data-height=&quot;1920&quot; style=&quot;z-index:1&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;!-- s02: data-start = &lt;d1 - transition duration&gt;, z-index 2 --&gt;\n  &lt;audio id=&quot;mix&quot; class=&quot;clip&quot; src=&quot;assets/audio/mix.wav&quot; data-start=&quot;0&quot; data-track-index=&quot;9&quot; data-volume=&quot;1&quot;&gt;&lt;/audio&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;script&gt;window.__timelines[&quot;main&quot;] = mgp.motion.timeline({ paused: true });&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>&lt;!-- compositions/s01.html --&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n&lt;style&gt;\n  #root{position:absolute;inset:0;overflow:hidden}\n  #s01-sky{position:absolute;inset:0;background:linear-gradient(#2C67B0,#78ACDC 33%,#D9E8F2 61%)}\n  .s01-pc{position:absolute;left:0;top:0;transform-origin:0 0;opacity:0;filter:drop-shadow(0 6px 10px rgba(45,28,12,.32))}\n  #s01-grain{position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%;mix-blend-mode:multiply;opacity:.10}\n&lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;div id=&quot;root&quot; data-composition-id=&quot;s01&quot; data-width=&quot;1080&quot; data-height=&quot;1920&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;div id=&quot;s01-sky&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div id=&quot;s01-world&quot; data-layout-allow-overflow&gt;\n    &lt;img id=&quot;s01-cloud1&quot; class=&quot;s01-pc&quot; src=&quot;assets/pieces/k1/cloud1.png&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;img id=&quot;s01-walk1&quot; class=&quot;s01-pc&quot; src=&quot;assets/seq/walk/walk1.png&quot;&gt;&lt;!-- one img per piece/frame; order = layering --&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;img id=&quot;s01-grain&quot; src=&quot;assets/fx/grain_1080x1920.png&quot; data-layout-ignore&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;script&gt;\nconst SC = {/* T6 data */}, S = 1080 / 720, q = t =&gt; Math.floor(t * 12) / 12;   // on twos\nconst ent = (t, t0) =&gt; t &lt; t0 ? null : Math.exp(-(t - t0) / 0.17);\nconst zk = (z, k) =&gt; Math.pow(z, 0.25 + 0.75 * k);\nconst boil = (f, i, n, a) =&gt; Math.sin(f * 12.9898 + i * 78.233 + n) * (a || 0);\nconst cl = u =&gt; Math.min(1, Math.max(0, u)), sm = u =&gt; (u = cl(u), u * u * (3 - 2 * u));\nfunction key(K, t, e) { let i = 0; while (i &lt; K.length - 2 &amp;&amp; t &gt; K[i + 1].t) i++;\n  const a = K[i], b = K[i + 1], u = e((t - a.t) / (b.t - a.t)), o = {};\n  for (const p in a) if (p !== &quot;t&quot;) o[p] = a[p] + (b[p] - a[p]) * u; return o; }\nfunction place(el, L, t, f, i, c, m) {\n  const e = ent(t, L.t0);\n  if (e === null || (L.t1 != null &amp;&amp; t &gt;= L.t1)) return mgp.motion.set(el, { opacity: 0 });\n  const o = (L.off || 0) * e, s = L.from === &quot;pop&quot; ? 1 - 0.08 * e : 1;\n  const dx = L.from === &quot;l&quot; ? -o : L.from === &quot;r&quot; ? o : 0, dy = L.from === &quot;b&quot; ? o : L.from === &quot;t&quot; ? -o : 0;\n  const [px, py] = SC.pivot, z = zk(c.z, L.k), sz = L.scale * s * z, [ax, ay] = m.anchor || [m.w / 2, m.h];\n  const X = px + (L.cx + dx + boil(f, i, 1, L.boil) - px) * z + c.x * L.k;\n  const Y = py + (L.bottom + dy + boil(f, i, 2, L.boil) - py) * z, fd = L.fade ?? 0.2;\n  mgp.motion.set(el, { x: S * (X - ax * sz), y: S * (Y - ay * sz), scale: S * sz, opacity: fd &gt; 0 ? cl((t - L.t0) / fd) : 1 });\n}\nfunction draw(tr) {\n  const t = q(tr), f = Math.floor(tr * 12), c = key(SC.camera, t, sm);\n  SC.layers.forEach((L, i) =&gt; {\n    if (!L.seq) return place(document.getElementById(&quot;s01-&quot; + L.id), L, t, f, i, c, SC.meta[L.id]);\n    const k = Math.max(0, Math.floor((t - L.t0) * 12)), A = L.seq.intro, B = L.seq.loop;\n    const cur = k &lt; A.length ? A[k] : B[(k - A.length) % B.length];\n    const M = Object.assign({}, L, L.keys ? key(L.keys, t, cl) : {});\n    [...new Set(A.concat(B))].forEach(n =&gt; { const el = document.getElementById(&quot;s01-&quot; + n);\n      n === cur ? place(el, M, t, f, i, c, SC.meta[n]) : mgp.motion.set(el, { opacity: 0 }); });\n  });\n}\ndocument.fonts.ready.then(() =&gt; {\n  const tl = mgp.motion.timeline({ paused: true }), P = { t: 0 };\n  tl.to(P, { t: SC.dur, duration: SC.dur, ease: &quot;none&quot;, onUpdate: () =&gt; draw(P.t) }, 0);\n  /* T8 text tweens go here */\n  draw(0);\n  window.__timelines[&quot;s01&quot;] = tl;\n});\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>After writing: <code>mgp check index.html<\/code> (or <code>--json<\/code>), <code>mgp frame<\/code> in the middle of the scene. Warm light (3.2) = a radial-gradient div above the world; if <code>drop-shadow<\/code> causes trouble, bake the shadow into the PNG.<\/p>"},{"id":"T8","title":"Kinetic typography","html":"<p>Markup + CSS go into the T7 scene, JS at the T7 comment (size 1080 w).<\/p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>&lt;style&gt;\n  .s01-card{position:absolute;left:66px;top:180px;padding:30px 39px;background:#FBF6EA;\n    box-shadow:-6px 8px 0 #E3B04B,0 9px 18px rgba(43,26,12,.32)}\n  .s01-kick{font:600 27px Poppins;letter-spacing:.3em;color:rgba(20,20,20,.75)}\n  .s01-head{font:700 75px/1.15 Poppins;color:#141414}\n  .s01-w{display:inline-block;opacity:0}\n  .s01-kw{position:relative;isolation:isolate;font-family:&quot;Playfair Display&quot;;font-style:italic;font-size:1.12em}\n  .s01-hl{position:absolute;left:-12px;right:-12px;top:34%;height:58%;z-index:-1;background:#F1C75B;\n    transform-origin:0 50%;clip-path:polygon(0 10%,100% 0,98% 100%,1% 90%)}\n&lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;div id=&quot;s01-card&quot; class=&quot;s01-card&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;s01-kick&quot;&gt;TIPS HARIAN&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;div class=&quot;s01-head&quot;&gt;&lt;span id=&quot;s01-w0&quot; class=&quot;s01-w&quot;&gt;Mulai&lt;/span&gt;\n  &lt;span id=&quot;s01-w1&quot; class=&quot;s01-w s01-kw&quot;&gt;&lt;i id=&quot;s01-hl1&quot; class=&quot;s01-hl&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;sekarang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n&lt;!-- sample on-screen text in Indonesian: &quot;TIPS HARIAN&quot; = DAILY TIPS, &quot;Mulai sekarang&quot; = Start now --&gt;<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>const F = 1 / 24, T = SC.texts[0];\ntl.fromTo(&quot;#s01-card&quot;, { x: -630, rotation: -1.2, opacity: 0 },\n  { x: 0, rotation: -1.2, opacity: 1, duration: 0.6, ease: &quot;expo.out&quot; }, T.t0);\nT.words.forEach(w =&gt; {\n  tl.fromTo(&quot;#&quot; + w.id, { opacity: 0, y: 12 }, { opacity: 1, y: 0, duration: 5 * F, ease: &quot;power2.out&quot; }, w.t - F);\n  if (!w.key) return;\n  tl.fromTo(&quot;#&quot; + w.hl, { scaleX: 0 }, { scaleX: 1, duration: 6 * F, ease: &quot;power2.out&quot; }, w.t + F);\n  tl.fromTo(&quot;#&quot; + w.id, { scale: 1 }, { scale: 1.055, duration: 3 * F, ease: &quot;sine.out&quot;,\n    yoyo: true, repeat: 1 }, w.t - 3 * F);                       // pulse peak at w.t\n});\ntl.to(&quot;#s01-card&quot;, { opacity: 0, duration: 0.18, ease: &quot;power2.in&quot; }, T.t1 - 0.18);\ntl.set(&quot;#s01-card&quot;, { opacity: 0 }, T.t1);\n// underline (instead of a strip): dash path from chapter 9.3, dashoffset 110 to 0 over 12 * F starting at w.t + 4 * F<\/code><\/pre><\/div>"},{"id":"T9","title":"Review checklist + contact sheet","html":"<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>No<\/th><th>Check<\/th><th>How<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>1<\/td><td>Clean pieces: no white fringe, grey boxes, specks or magenta edges<\/td><td><code>mgp frame --scale 2<\/code>, then crop the edges on a contrasting background<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>2<\/td><td>Feet planted, contact shadow, one light direction<\/td><td>Crop the feet in every pose<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>3<\/td><td>No AI text; geography and screen directions correct<\/td><td>Crop; location line<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>4<\/td><td>Hero around 70%, at least 3 planes, props with context<\/td><td>Hold frame<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>5<\/td><td>On twos, stagger, settle 14-16 frames, hold at least 25%<\/td><td><code>mgp contact --from &lt;a&gt; --to &lt;b&gt; --n 8<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>6<\/td><td>Words at their VO timestamps, hold 0.5 s + 0.3 s/word, safe zone<\/td><td>A frame per word<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>7<\/td><td>Arabic: U+FDF4/U+FDFA render, harakat match the source<\/td><td>Crop<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>8<\/td><td>Layer edges hidden; same character size; no foot sliding; mouth/brows on the face<\/td><td>Frames at the camera extremes; 3 consecutive frames<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>9<\/td><td>Clean transitions at 25/50/75% (per block)<\/td><td><code>mgp frame --t &lt;time&gt;<\/code> at three times<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>10<\/td><td>Dead air: silence + static &gt; 0.8 s<\/td><td>silencedetect + freezedetect<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>11<\/td><td>-14 LUFS, -1.5 dBTP, SFX -5 dB under the VO<\/td><td>loudnorm summary<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>12<\/td><td>Neighbouring scenes differ in archetype/colour; data and proof texts sourced<\/td><td>Block contact sheet; <code>sources<\/code><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>13<\/td><td><code>mgp check --json<\/code> 0 ERROR; duration, fps, resolution correct; placeholders labelled<\/td><td>CLI; ffprobe<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>14<\/td><td>Branch B: the still layer composite = the keyframe (side by side)<\/td><td>Comparison sheet<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>15<\/td><td>Face-bearing object: mood changes through anticipation, squash, new face, settle; eyes lead, parts not in unison<\/td><td><code>mgp contact --from --to<\/code> around the mood change, 8 frames<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>16<\/td><td>Character matches the model sheet (proportions, colours, costume); same character height across scenes and episodes<\/td><td>Sprite and model sheet side by side at the same scale<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>17<\/td><td>Lip-sync: m/b/p closed, vowels match the letters, X in pauses; stickers on the face<\/td><td>A frame at every m/b/p word in the block + clip with sound<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>18<\/td><td>Code-drawn style: the STYLE card is followed, the forbidden list is clean<\/td><td>Side by side with the approved style frame<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>19<\/td><td>Motion curves: no accidental linear motion; breaks (pops) only at impacts/cuts; squash with constant volume; no abrupt start/stop without a reason<\/td><td><code>mgp curves index.html --from &lt;a&gt; --to &lt;b&gt; -o review/curves.png<\/code> (benchmarks: <code>motion-graphics/references/advanced-principles.md<\/code> chapter 13 and the benchmark table)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>20<\/td><td>Blocking approved before spline: key poses read, staging and rough timing right<\/td><td><code>mgp contact index.html --var blocking=true --times poses -o review/blocking.png<\/code><\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>ffmpeg -i review.mp4 -vf &quot;fps=2,scale=360:-1,tile=6x4&quot; -frames:v 1 sheet_s03.jpg\nffmpeg -i review.mp4 -af silencedetect=noise=-35dB:d=0.8 -vf freezedetect=n=0.003:d=0.8 -f null - 2&gt;&amp;1 | grep -E &quot;silence_|freeze_&quot;\nffmpeg -i final.mp4 -af loudnorm=I=-14:TP=-1.5:LRA=11:print_format=summary -f null -<\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Routine: a contact sheet per shot (<code>mgp contact<\/code>, 8 frames) and per block (2 fps), then a full-res crop at the contact points. Pilot: the blocking sheet (<code>--times poses<\/code>) first, then curves (<code>mgp curves<\/code>) after the spline. <code>mgp audit &lt;folder&gt;<\/code> runs check, curves, safe zones, VO, loudness, <code>project.json<\/code> and asset licences in one report (<code>review/audit.md<\/code>, T9-style table).<\/p>"},{"id":"T9a","title":"QC references per asset type (REPORT conditions)","html":"<p>Checks from other skills are not copied; Claude opens the referenced chapter when that asset type is used. P0 = breaks readability or the contract (chapter 12); REPORT only when every P0 passes.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Asset type in the scene<\/th><th>Checks run<\/th><th>P0<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Paper pieces, sets, branch B layers<\/td><td>T9 1-4, 14<\/td><td>T9 1, 3, 14<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Sequence-sheet character<\/td><td>T9 2, 5, 8, 16 + <code>motion-graphics<\/code> chapter 9 items 1 (no foot sliding, 3 consecutive frames), 4 (loop without jumps), 9 (not in unison, last frame not dead)<\/td><td>T9 2, 8, 16; mg 9 items 1, 4<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Vector rig character<\/td><td><code>vector-character<\/code> chapter 11 items 1-32 + <code>motion-graphics<\/code> chapter 9 items 0-16<\/td><td>vc 11 items 9, 17; mg 9 items 0, 1, 3<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Face-bearing object<\/td><td>T9 8, 15, 16, 17<\/td><td>T9 8, 15<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>New code-drawn style<\/td><td>T9 18<\/td><td>T9 18<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Maps and routes<\/td><td>Chapter 9 + T9 3, 12<\/td><td>Sourced coordinates; map aspect ratio not stretched<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Text, proof texts, Arabic<\/td><td>T9 6, 7, 12<\/td><td>T9 7; proof texts sourced<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Audio<\/td><td>T9 10, 11<\/td><td>T9 11<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Blocks, transitions, render<\/td><td>T9 9, 13<\/td><td>T9 13<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Motion after spline (G4 pilot and new blocks)<\/td><td>T9 5, 19, 20 + benchmarks in <code>motion-graphics/references/advanced-principles.md<\/code><\/td><td>T9 20 (pilot); an unintended curve finding = P1<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>\n<p>REPORT format:<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Block/shot<\/th><th>Asset type<\/th><th>Checks run<\/th><th>Result (numbers)<\/th><th>P0<\/th><th>Visible weaknesses<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>&lt;b2 s05&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;type&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;check numbers&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;check result numbers&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;pass/fail&gt;<\/td><td>&lt;write honestly&gt;<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>"},{"id":"T10","title":"Handover","html":"<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"markdown\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code># Handover &lt;project&gt; &lt;episode&gt;\n## Status &lt;date time&gt;\nStage: &lt;number + name&gt;. Last gate: G&lt;n&gt; (&lt;waiting/passed date&gt;). Locked blocks: &lt;list&gt;. Running block: &lt;name&gt;.\nEnvironment: &lt;Claude Code / own computer: render works | claude.ai: without Node 22+, Chrome/Chromium or ffmpeg there is no render, so render in Claude Code or on your own computer&gt;.\n## Review Results &lt;date time&gt; (PLAN, not done yet)\n1. &lt;seconds/shot&gt;: &lt;the user&#x27;s request, in the user&#x27;s words when it matters&gt;\n## Update &lt;date time&gt; - done\n- Files, changes, commands, deliveries: &lt;path, key numbers, render/snapshot, new file (range)&gt;\n## Higgsfield assets\nT5 table + target files; balance &lt;remaining&gt; (tool balance, &lt;date time&gt;).\n## Motion library\nNew actions: &lt;character, action, job, passed T9a on date&gt;.\n## Next steps and open questions\n1. &lt;step&gt;; &lt;options + cost, waiting for the user&gt;\n## Opening prompt for the next session\n&quot;Continue &lt;project&gt; &lt;episode&gt;. Read the motion-graphics-pro skill, then the last section of &lt;handover path&gt; first. Stage: &lt;stage&gt;, block &lt;name&gt;. Task: &lt;task&gt;. Higgsfield only after permission + a balance check; full render only after I write &#x27;render&#x27;.&quot;<\/code><\/pre><\/div>"},{"id":"T11","title":"Branch B keyframe (9:16 2K)","html":"<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>CANVAS: Vertical 9:16 handcrafted paper collage diorama, &lt;camera, vanishing point in percent&gt;. Built from clearly separated depth layers with small gaps between groups, so each layer can be cut out later.\nLAYER PLAN (frame terms, percent of frame): 1. &lt;sky/roof, y 0-35%&gt;. 2. &lt;end of the alley, x 40-60%, y 30-45%&gt;. 3. &lt;left row, x 0-40%&gt;. 4. &lt;right row, x 60-100%&gt;. 5. &lt;open floor, y 45-100%, centre path empty&gt;. 6. &lt;background people, area, facing direction&gt;. 7. &lt;front-left props&gt;. 8. &lt;front-right props&gt;.\nDETAIL: medium detail; small goods grouped in clusters; foreground objects cover at most a third of what is behind them.\nSTYLE: &lt;STYLE block 4.3&gt;.\nONLY: Show only what this prompt describes; add nothing else.<\/code><\/pre><\/div>"},{"id":"T12","title":"Layer-separation sheet (21:9 2K, keyframe attached)","html":"<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>Layer-separation sheet. COPY from the attached keyframe, do not redraw. Wide 21:9 sheet, four equal vertical 9:16 panels separated by thin white gutters; inside each panel the background is flat solid magenta #FF00FF with no texture, shadow or outline. Every panel uses exactly the keyframe&#x27;s framing: same scale, position, pose and facing direction for everything that is kept.\nPanel 1 KEEP &lt;layer + area in percent&gt;. ERASE &lt;objects removed&gt;. FILL &lt;back layer: continue only this surface; other layers: no fill&gt;.\nPanel 2 KEEP ... ERASE ... FILL ...\nPanel 3 KEEP ... ERASE ... FILL ...\nPanel 4 KEEP ... ERASE ... FILL ...\nKeep the original paper textures, colours and light. No readable text, letters, numbers, symbols, logos or watermarks.<\/code><\/pre><\/div>"},{"id":"T13","title":"Motion set (21:9 2K)","html":"<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy<\/button><pre><code>Stop-motion frame set on a flat plain pure white background: &lt;N&gt; frames of &lt;object/character&gt; in one row, identical size and position in every frame, same baseline, white gutters between frames; only &lt;the moving part&gt; changes: F1 &lt;...&gt;, F2 &lt;...&gt;, F3 &lt;...&gt;. STYLE: &lt;STYLE block 4.3&gt;. ONLY: Show only these frames; add nothing else.<\/code><\/pre><\/div>"},{"id":"T14","title":"Pixel art (sprites, limited palette) (Version 2.1)","html":"<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field<\/th><th>Rule<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Native canvas<\/td><td>Draw at a small resolution, e.g. 160 x 90 (16:9) or 90 x 160 (9:16); characters about 32-48 pixels tall.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Palette<\/td><td>8-16 fixed colours, written as a hex list; every pixel uses one of them, no smooth gradients.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Lines and shadows<\/td><td>1-pixel dark outline; shadows one colour step; sky gradients use checkerboard dither.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Sprite sheet<\/td><td>One row per action: idle 2-4 drawings, walk 6-8, jump 4-6; same cell size, same foot point.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Motion<\/td><td>8-12 drawings per second (stepped, no interpolation); positions and camera always on whole pixels.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Upscale<\/td><td>Whole-number scale (x3, x4, x6) with nearest neighbour; never smoothed or freely rotated.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Check<\/td><td>No mixed pixels at edges, no half pixels, no extra palette colours, no foot sliding.<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>"},{"id":"T15","title":"Origami (folded paper, fold-unfold) (Version 2.1)","html":"<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field<\/th><th>Rule<\/th><\/tr><\/thead><tbody><tr><td>Shape<\/td><td>Every object and character is built from flat planes (triangles, kites) with clear fold lines.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Paper colour<\/td><td>One colour per sheet; a light side and a dark side per fold (10-20% apart); the paper's back side is cream.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Fold lines<\/td><td>Thin lines, lighter on mountain folds, darker on valley folds; no thick ink lines.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Background<\/td><td>Finely textured kraft paper, mountains and trees of folded paper, soft shadows under the layers.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Fold-unfold<\/td><td>Planes turn on their fold line one at a time (0.02-0.05 s apart), 0.4-0.6 s per plane; the back side shows as it turns.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Motion example<\/td><td>Crane: square, triangle, bird base, finished crane; hold each step 0.5 s, small wing flaps at the end.<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Motion<\/td><td>12 drawings per second so it feels like paper stop motion.<\/td><\/tr><\/tbody><\/table><\/div>"}],"title":"Motion Graphics Pro: paper collage + mgp","intro":"<p>Built from the author's <code>papercut-mv<\/code> and <code>motion-graphics<\/code> methods, public research (principles only; credits in the package root <code>NOTICE.md<\/code>) and real production tests. Rendering uses <strong>mgp<\/strong> (<code>render/<\/code>, old name Rekam), the package's own local renderer; compositions are compatible with the HyperFrames HTML format, but no third-party skill or CLI is needed. Version history: <code>references/history.md<\/code>.<\/p>\n<p>Units: pixels on a short side of 720 (9:16 = 720 x 1280); for a short side of 1080 (1080 x 1920, 1920 x 1080, 1080 x 1080, 1080 x 1350, 2520 x 1080) multiply by S = 1.5; 4K (short side 2160) S = 3. Time in seconds (s), 24 fps. <code>mgp<\/code> = <code>node \"${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}/render/bin/mgp.mjs\"<\/code> (this skill's folder). If <code>${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}<\/code> appears literally (not substituted, e.g. a plain skill install), use the folder this SKILL.md is in. Shell variables do not carry it, so write the full path into each command.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Environment.<\/strong> The package installs as the Claude plugin <code>motion-graphics-pro<\/code> (skills <code>motion-graphics-pro<\/code>, <code>motion-graphics<\/code>, <code>vector-character<\/code> side by side under <code>skills/<\/code>) or as three plain skill folders; mgp runs in place in both and finds the sibling <code>motion-graphics/lib<\/code> itself. Treat the skill folder as read-only: projects, renders and reviews go in the user's working or output folder; voice profiles and installed engines live in the mgp home (<code>~/.mgp<\/code>), never inside the plugin, which an update replaces. Rendering needs Node 22+, Chrome/Chromium and ffmpeg (<code>node --version<\/code>, <code>ffmpeg -version<\/code>, <code>mgp doctor<\/code>). When one is missing (common in the claude.ai chat sandbox), still do the brief, concept, script, assets and composition code (and <code>mgp new/add/catalog<\/code> when Node runs), and tell the user plainly to render in Claude Code or on their own computer. Do not claim a render ran.<\/p>\n<p>This file is the core: flow, gates, required rules, key checks and the reference map. Details (numbers, recipes, prompts, templates) live in <code>references/*.md<\/code> and are read when their step comes (table \"References\" at the end). Old chapter numbers (e.g. \"chapter 6.4\", \"T9a\") are still used in the reference files.<\/p>"});
